[{"id":"doc_1785212350055_7ajtlt","name":"basquiat_real_story","text":"you can say something right now and would inspired me to write a song or something to happen you know most Kings just happen to be inspired by BOS drawing you know the drawing and he had the most young kings get their head cut off on the bottom and I looked at that and I was like it's powerful Jean Michelle bosia is a name that reverberates through the art World graffiti artists turn art sensation bosia is one of the most iconic and enigmatic artists of the 20th century his work challenged the conventional boundaries of Art and his paintings are some of the top selling contemporary pieces being sold auctions today in May of 2017 his Untitled painting sold for 110.5 million to Japanese billionaire yaku maawa which at the time was the priciest work ever sold by a us [Music] artist Washington Bridge in Queens the inbound La is slow from the Grand Central in through Queens Boulevard the Grand Central slugish Francis Lewis in through 188 then pretty much from the BOS referenced fine artists such as Jean dupet and S twamley writers like Alan Ginsburg and William Burrows and musicians like David Bowie and Miles Davis he would look at how early rappers at the time would use samples in their music and he would do the same with his art in the same way artists would borrow from James Brown he'd borrow from Picasso his reference points were many an Eclectic as he'd paint with the TV on music blasting and with reference books all around him we asked bosia about the words in his paintings where did the the words come from I um real life books to television yeah and you you just skim them and start including no me when I'm working I hear them you know and I just throw them down oh yeah well I mean things like Punic Wars I remember that was in one of your oh that was from uh a a guide book on Roman history using inspiration from poetry Anatomy typography trash TV and so on this allowed him to create visual collages that were incredibly unique his work was filled with cultural references and esoteric illusions taking on politics pop culture racism and art history he's been referenced by numerous hip-hop artists including Kanye JayZ Nas and J Cole his rise came extremely fast as he managed to squeeze a lifetime worth of work in just 10 years time as he tragically passed away at just 27 years old bad's Journey Begins in Brooklyn New York in 1960 born to a Haitian father and Puerto Rican mother he had a multicultural upbringing which heavily influenced his art Basia experienced adversity very early on in life when he was just seven years old he was hit by a car while playing in the street he broke his arm and suffered several internal injuries causing him to require a surgery to remove his spleen while he was hospitalized his mother bought him the book raise Anatomy which would ultimately influence his art going forward I wanted to do some Anatomy stuff uh and and I went I B some books that about Anatomy and then you started imitating well not really imitating cuz you know but I I used them as a as a source material he mainly grew up with his father primarily due to his mother's mental state as she was in and out of Mental Hospitals for most of her adulthood overall he was a gifted kid as he learned to read and write by the age of four and was fluent in English Spanish and French the family bounced around a bit during his early years first living in Brooklyn then moving to Puerto Rico for 2 years before settling back in the BK and he would attend Edward Moro high school during these years he really struggled due to his mother's mental instability and he began to Rebel he would often skip school he ran away from home slept on park benches in Washington Square Park and he even got expelled for pranking his principal he would eventually drop out all together at 17 which caused his strict father to kick him out of the house in 1976 Basia met Al Diaz at cityz school high school the two bonded over their academic problems and Puerto Rican Heritage in 1978 they began to spray paint graffiti on buildings all over Lower Manhattan working under the pseudonym same the phrase was something the two made up short for same old [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] the tag would usually accompany the copyright symbol along with short phrases which served as poetic and satirical advertising slogans same old tags were different from other tags at the time because there was always a message attached to it a deeper form of expressionism as opposed to people just writing their names me and B were in school together I I came from High School of Art and Design and we met met at at a city as a school Alternative High School where it wasn't the usual classroom format it was you go out and do stuff you know like actually be involved and stuff and the same was started out as a private joke it came from from the expression same o same like like you hear two older black guys you know talking and say hey what's up every one guy will say same same you know same same old [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] sort of thing it was more about the tamber the sound of of the the the term so we we like play with it on December 11th 1978 the tags would get their first taste of public recognition The Village Voice news and culture paper would publish an article about the same old graffiti going around and then an interview with both Jean and Al after dropping out of school bosia was heavily active in the Lower East Side including the East Village Soho and around the School of Visual Arts this is where he would be friend fellow artist Keith Herring and Kenny sharf in hindsight this was a legendary crew considering the notoriety and impact that collectively made in the art World artist Keith Herring had been following the same old graffiti and befriended bosot that year hering recalled I still hadn't met Jam Melle I had only heard about him well one day a kid came up to me just as I was going into SVA and he asked if I could walk him through past the security guard he wanted to get inside the school I said sure and we walked through I Disappeared into a class when I came out an hour later I noticed there were all these fresh same old poems and tags in places that they hadn't been an hour ago I put two and two together and realized that the person I had walked through was bosia in 1979 19-year-old bosia appeared on the show TV party hosted by Glenn O'Brien and revealed himself as same he and O'Brien would develop a close relationship and he would make regular appearances on the show [Music] afterward show Melle all right so tonight after this really good meal you know I went I want to go to the movies where i' see saxi driver and then when I got there I put my $10 in the window the lady disappeared from it and came back gave me back my $10 and said no music man this is serious this a mistake so I was like denied entrance to a movie theater you know why why which just they said it was my appearance you know that look like a bum you know that same year BOS met Michael Homan at the Canal Zone party and together they formed the rock band gray they performed at various clubs around the city including Max's Kansas City CBGB and the Mud Club around this time bosia I moved in with Alexis Adler who lived in the East Village section of Lura Manhattan Adler who was a biology graduate at Barnard saw firsthand his creative Explorations he couldn't afford canvases so instead he used the floors walls doors and Furniture to work on he'd also create postcards and sell them around the city for a dollar while selling these postcards in SoHo he saw his Idol Andy Warhol at the WPA restaurant in Manhattan's Spring Street with friend and curator Henry geler geler dismissed young basat initially but Warhol ignored it and bought one of his postcards titled stupid games bad ideas this would be their first encounter but did they know that this would eventually lead into a full-on professional relationship later that year bosia began working on O'Brien's independent film downtown 81 the feature film wouldn't actually get released until the year 2000 almost 20 years later he was homeless at the time of the movie and slept in the production office the film vividly depicts life in Manhattan's downtown explosive art and music scene between 1980 and 1981 bat's first public exhibition was at the Time Square show in New York in June of 1980 where Jeffrey D was the first first to mention him in a press article titled report from Time Square a lot of people thought that Jean Michelle came from the ghetto that he was a real tough Street kid Jean Michelle didn't come from the ghetto but he was a real tough Street kid he left home around age 15 and basically lived on the streets of grenwich Village he slept in Washington Square Park his his father in fact told me a story about coming to to find him uh and up finding him in Washington Square Park and trying to bring him back home well like a lot of artists John Michelle reinvented himself uh but what was extraordinary about Jean Michelle is that he didn't wait until he was 30 years old to do it he did it when he was 15 16 17 years old Jean Michelle was the type of person who would have ended up at the center no matter what but he had that instinct believe I first saw his work in his apartment with with Diego Cortez Diego had met him in February of 1981 he participated in the New York New Wave show at New York's PS1 curated by Diego Cortez 119 other artists were present including Keith Herring and Andy Warhol in 1981 he would meet art dealer anina nose nose took interest in his paintings early on she owned the gallery in SoHo and really wanted to represent him with his lack of resources she gave him cash to buy supplies and offered up the gallery's basement store room for him to use as his own Studio by ' 82 no would put on his first show which featured his canvas paintings all of which sold out Basia became an overnight [Music] success he'd eventually leave the nose gallery and gallerist Bruno Bishop Burger became his worldwide art dealer nose had represented bosia for about four to five months at this point but the main reason for him leaving was because despite his several warnings she sold multiple paintings of his that that to him were finished Bishop Burger would then become his worldwide exclusive art dealer until his untimely death and gave bosia his own oneman show at his Gallery in Zurich so in a very short amount of time bosia went from being a homeless relatively unknown artist to becoming an absolute superstar that same year in October of 1982 Bishop Berger would arrange a lunch for him to meet none other than Andy Warhol at his Studio turn nightclub called the factory the two immediately hit it off Warhol brought his Polaroid camera and began taking pictures of the young artist then they took a few together then out of nowhere basad decided to leave before they even finish lunch he returned about an hour later with the fresh painting paint still wet of the two together and he named it do cesas visitors and employees flocked around to see the painting and it was easy to say that Warhol was impressed saying I'm really jealous he is faster than me this would officially Mark the beginning of their Infamous relationship the two began to work together frequently and created famous works such as Armen Hammer 2 and Untitled General Electric too it made for a timely combination as Warhol in the early 80s was struggling to maintain relevancy he was considered a hasb been and bosia was a young upand cominging Talent Fresh eyes and a away for Warhol to stay in touch with the new generation unfortunately the relationship was shortlived it all started at the Tony shafrazi gallery show in 1985 the critical reaction to the show was poor with bosia being referred to as warhol's mascot in a New York Times article after the show it became an embarrassment for the pair to even be seen together bosia took a personal as he didn't want people to look at him as just another warhall Protegé eventually he would slowly stop calling warhall and then altogether the two would stop collaborating and the relationship never recovered from there on February 22nd 1987 Andy warhall passed away his heart stopped beating after he had been recovering from gallbladder surgery basad struggled to cope with his death his drug use continue to worson in 1986 his girlfriend Jennifer good was able to get him into a methadone program but he quit after after just 3 weeks in the last months of his life bosot became a recluse despite his attempts of sobriety bosot passed away at his home on August 12th 1988 from a heroin overdose he was found unresponsive in his home by his girlfriend Kelly imman the greatest black contemporary artist of his generation was Gone Too Soon at the young age of 27 his funeral will be held at the Greenwood Cemetery in Brooklyn in the obituary Herring wrote for Vogue he stated he truly created a lifetime of Works in 10 years greedily we wonder what else he might have created what masterpieces we have been cheated out of by his death but the fact is that he has created enough work to Intrigue generations to come only now will people begin to understand the magnitude of his contribution bosia left the world with over a thousand paintings and drawings after his death the New York Times indicated that bosia was the most famous of only a small number of young black artists who have achieved National recognition his stamp on pop culture is unparalleled from references by musicians to his influence and fashion Basia was able to leave his mark in the world contributing the best way he knew how from rebellious Street kid to a legend in the art World a leader in the neoexpressionism movement Jean Michelle's work will continue to reverberate Through Time","uploadedAt":"2026-07-28T04:19:10.055Z","chunks":[{"id":"doc_1785212350055_7ajtlt_c0","text":"you can say something right 