 was fluent in English Spanish and French the family bounced around a bit during his early years first living in Brooklyn then moving to Puerto Rico for 2 years before settling back in the BK and he would attend Edward Moro high school during these years he really struggled due to his mother's mental instability and he began to Rebel he would often skip school he ran away from home slept on park benches in Washington Square Park and he even got expelled for pranking his principal he would eventually drop out all together at 17 which caused his strict father to kick him out of the house in 1976 Basia met Al Diaz at cityz school high school the two bonded over their academic problems and Puerto Rican Heritage in 1978 they began to spray paint graffiti on buildings all over Lower Manhattan working under the pseudonym same the phrase was something the two made up short for same old [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] the tag would usually accompany the copyright symbol along with short phrases which served as poetic and satirical advertising slogans same old tags were different from other tags at the time because there was always a message attached to it a deeper form of expressionism as opposed to people just writing their names me and B were in school together I I came from High School of Art and Design and we met met at at a city as a school Alternative High School where it wasn't the usual classroom format it was you go out and do stuff you know like actually be involved and stuff and the same was started out as a private joke it came from from the expression same o same like like you hear two older black guys you know talking and say hey what's up every one guy will say same same you know same same old [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] sort of thing it was more about the tamber the sound of of the the the term so we we like play with it on December 11th 1978 the tags would get their first taste of public recognition The Village Voice news and culture paper would publish an article about the same old 