 that he was a real tough Street kid Jean Michelle didn't come from the ghetto but he was a real tough Street kid he left home around age 15 and basically lived on the streets of grenwich Village he slept in Washington Square Park his his father in fact told me a story about coming to to find him uh and up finding him in Washington Square Park and trying to bring him back home well like a lot of artists John Michelle reinvented himself uh but what was extraordinary about Jean Michelle is that he didn't wait until he was 30 years old to do it he did it when he was 15 16 17 years old Jean Michelle was the type of person who would have ended up at the center no matter what but he had that instinct believe I first saw his work in his apartment with with Diego Cortez Diego had met him in February of 1981 he participated in the New York New Wave show at New York's PS1 curated by Diego Cortez 119 other artists were present including Keith Herring and Andy Warhol in 1981 he would meet art dealer anina nose nose took interest in his paintings early on she owned the gallery in SoHo and really wanted to represent him with his lack of resources she gave him cash to buy supplies and offered up the gallery's basement store room for him to use as his own Studio by ' 82 no would put on his first show which featured his canvas paintings all of which sold out Basia became an overnight [Music] success he'd eventually leave the nose gallery and gallerist Bruno Bishop Burger became his worldwide art dealer nose had represented bosia for about four to five months at this point but the main reason for him leaving was because despite his several warnings she sold multiple paintings of his that that to him were finished Bishop Burger would then become his worldwide exclusive art dealer until his untimely death and gave bosia his own oneman show at his Gallery in Zurich so in a very short amount of time bosia went from being a homeless relatively unknown artist to becoming an absolute superstar that same year in October of 1982 Bishop Berger would arrange a lunch for him to meet none other than Andy Warhol at his Studio turn nightclub called the factory the two immediately hit it off Warhol brought his Polaroid camera and began taking pictures of the","t0":600,"t1":800,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"15":0.07269073368507865,"16":0.03634536684253933,"17":0.03748799227194107,"30":0.03634536684253933,"82":0.053799577979497276,"119":0.053799577979497276,"1980":0.09880468982778967,"1981":0.14820703474168448,"1982":0.049402344913894836,"homeless":0.09256491680629189,"time":0.07143010110153383,"movie":0.03876533882679461,"slept":0.08772497283778131,"production":0.03748799227194107,"office":0.041885225337543505,"film":0.0353117364123414,"vividly":0.046282458403145946,"depicts":0.04021347491348312,"life":0.019562188960319773,"manhattan":0.041885225337543505,"downtown":0.04386248641889066,"explosive":0.046282458403145946,"art":0.08417335400245475,"music":0.04431322735797979,"scene":0.04386248641889066,"between":0.0319481337769369,"bat":0.049402344913894836,"first":0.12037705742975945,"public":0.03748799227194107,"exhibition":0.03748799227194107,"square":0.18512983361258378,"show":0.10229455851994924,"york":0.10050014912850659,"june":0.046282458403145946,"where":0.018891155705581385,"jeffrey":0.049402344913894836,"mention":0.053799577979497276,"him":0.26482952634547074,"press":0.053799577979497276,"article":0.041885225337543505,"titled":0.049402344913894836,"report":0.053799577979497276,"lot":0.042622255379673325,"people":0.015717179750365278,"thought":0.030590750614277046,"jean":0.1608538996539325,"michelle":0.21931243209445328,"came":0.030590750614277046,"ghetto":0.10759915595899455,"he":0.17567392923166272,"real":0.05196586026630173,"tough":0.10759915595899455,"street":0.08042694982696624,"kid":0.07497598454388214,"didn":0.05196586026630173,"come":0.02479867351583241,"left":0.03634536684253933,"home":0.0638962675538738,"around":0.024075411485951888,"age":0.0326