rt World a leader in the neoexpressionism movement Jean Michelle's work will continue to reverberate Through Time","t0":1000,"t1":1013,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"contributing":0.27779470609252144,"best":0.16116390686379792,"way":0.11083474373171208,"he":0.08786858135671656,"knew":0.21815069571070572,"rebellious":0.29652076313871756,"street":0.2413676980432547,"kid":0.22500891600163397,"legend":0.2632696478023456,"art":0.12630547805096864,"world":0.10485872517089753,"leader":0.2632696478023456,"neoexpressionism":0.29652076313871756,"movement":0.22500891600163397,"jean":0.2413676980432547,"michelle":0.2632696478023456,"work":0.09938551220323681,"continue":0.2632696478023456,"reverberate":0.29652076313871756,"through":0.1488455984945432,"time":0.10718371833674517}}],"kind":"transcript","tsMode":"estimate"},{"id":"doc_1785212350089_40en7f","name":"kanye_7_principles","text":"um so for establishing purposes would you please introduce yourself what's up I'm Kanye West yeah well you know when you first start producing you can start from sampling you can start from programming a kick drum a hi-hat and a snare and then you know you you start from like you know my first beat to you know getting to the level of like the Masters that use sampling and you know they sample drums from the 70s and the 60s and you know mix it with some sample from the 80s and a little word sample from KRS or something on top of it you know or uh a James Brown scream and this combination is uh you know it's how people Define their textures and who they are I've grown for once let's go ah he doesn't stop my father asked me that question back when I was 15 I was really or maybe I was 16. he said I was I was a photographer um back in Atlanta I'll say that you know I was pretty good I wasn't one of the best but son what do you think you stand and I looked at him I said I am the best and this is 16 and that was a little delusional where do you think that came from this sense of from my grandfather my grandfather on my mother's side if you could hear him talk yeah he believed in himself he believed in himself and he believed in other people around him that believed in him what do you fear now what do I feel it just feels like being whack fear of not having an idea because at that point I'll be dead my ideas are my on my air I breathe creativity stuff in the city Tantrums based off the way we was branded it I feel like everything that anybody ever said in life would be a disadvantage to me imma make it my advantage when I was playing basketball everybody said I was too short I'm killing them with the Scoops you know what I'm saying everybody says You Can't Rap cause you're a producer okay oh I didn't hear that beat oh yeah I know I produced it I just wrapped on it before you got a chance to hit you know what I'm saying like I'm gonna use everything that everybody says that I can't do or and I'm gonna flip it to the positive like I look at everything as a glass half full and then half empty and it's like I'm the type of person I don't hold grudges like one of my best friends made a song dissing me and I looked at all the positive in the situation I'm like look that's probably my name or more he came to me like yo I'm sorry like I feel like maybe a lot of the rules of hip-hop like a lot of your aggression and the negativity that people have towards people maybe I'm not hip-hop because of that because of where my heart is because I won't confide to what people say a lot of stuff I feel like 90 10 of life is what happens to you 90 is how you react to it my my talent is the ability to learn if I could play you the tracks that I had when I was you know in seventh grade and compare that to Jesus walks All Falls Down stand up Overnight Celebrity hold on I'm not feeling I'm gonna name everything nominated slow jams but if I could play you that right now and show you that track and I'll go and shoot a jump shot right now miss it yeah it's the same thing as how my tracks were right back you know anything in life that you put your mind to you can do the world the world is your oyster that's all I tell people and I really have a grasp of that being that I nearly died I use Through the Wire and this entire album as um my Rehabilitation it was the first opportunity I had to tell people I'm not going to the studio and instead of staying at the hotel I snuck in and I made songs that inspired me songs that gave me life I feel like this album was kind of like my angel that helped heal me you know I couldn't eat I couldn't enjoy food there's a lot of things I couldn't do but one thing I could do is go to the studio and turn it up loud and that's the reason why a lot of the music on there are you know they they evoke a certain emotion they raise your spirits uh any situation you're going through from spaceships I've been working this great shift and I ain't made it I wish I could buy me a spaceship and fly and anybody that's in that situation they listen to the song and it'll be like this spaceship tell me this record's not hot even though the drums ain't right I still think I want to get the drums right and get the [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] knocking you know for the episode right now and you're gonna go crazy I'm gonna tell you something this young man does y'all they get up every morning and eat breakfast together this whole crew and they talk about yesterday and the next day and the day of and the present they plan on over breakfast they sit there and they talk about what they're going to do what they did and how to make the music better then they go exercise together okay go to the YMCA they play basketball lift weights Focus get the energy out get the chi up and then he goes to the studio and he stays there from four and when I was there we left it for so 12 hours of in the studio work then go to bed get up and do it again the next day the way everything happened though was like Focus energy yo and actually I never seen that from a laughable you know people can write right right all they want but what are people doing and if there's anybody that's out there doing they know how hard it is to actually do and I'm like a motivation of the doers if if everything I did failed just the fact that I'm willing to fail is an inspiration people are so scared to lose that they don't even try like one thing that people can't say is they can't say I'm not trying I'm not trying my hardest and I'm not trying to do it the best way that I know how what what little information I get all these people that you know fought so hard you know and that's what gives me like my fight just thinking like I have to work on behalf of Dilla I have to like when I put a weird ass [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] just Jamaican sample it's like it works at first but it's not till I put like that that it sounds like art or sounds slightly wrong and now we'll go to the radio now that it's wrong [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] now play this play this five minute song that completely [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] up your programming like play this you know that's you know that's in lineage of it's it's it's the best respect that we can pay to like great artists that have inspired us so much is to never [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] never sell out we gotta fight you know you we got to make music and we think like if Dylan was alive would you like this","uploadedAt":"2026-07-28T04:19:10.089Z","chunks":[{"id":"doc_1785212350089_40en7f_c0","text":"um so for establishing purposes would you please introduce yourself what's up I'm Kanye West yeah well you know when you first start producing you can start from sampling you can start from programming a kick drum a hi-hat and a snare and then you know you you start from like you know my first beat to you know getting to the level of like the Masters that use sampling and you know they sample drums from the 70s and the 60s and you know mix it with some sample from the 80s and a little word sample from KRS or something on top of it you know or uh a James Brown scream and this combination is uh you know it's how people Define their textures and who they are I've grown for once let's go ah he doesn't stop my father asked me that question back when I was 15 I was really or maybe I was 16. he said I was I was a photographer um back in Atlanta I'll say that you know I was pretty good I wasn't one of the best but son what do you think you stand and I looked at him I said I am the best and this is 16 and that was a little delusional where do you think that came from this sense of from my grandfather my grandfather on my mother's side if you could hear him talk yeah he believed in himself he believed in himself and he believed in other people around him that believed in him what do you fear now what do I feel it just feels like being whack fear of not having an idea because at that point I'll be dead my ideas are my on my air I breathe creativity stuff in the city Tantrums based off the way we was branded it I feel like everything that anybody ever said in life would be a disadvantage to me imma make it my advantage when I was playing basketball everybody said I was too short I'm killing them with the Scoops you know what I'm saying everybody says You Can't Rap cause you're a producer okay oh I didn't hear that beat oh yeah I know I produced it I just wrapped on it before you got a chance to hit you know what I'm saying like I'm gonna use everything that everybody says that I can't do or and I'm gonna flip it to the positive like I look at everything as a glass half full and then half empty and it's like I'm the type of person I don't hold grudges like one of my best friends made a song dissing me and I looked at all the positive in the situation I'm like look that's probably my name or more he came to me like yo I'm sorry like I feel like maybe a lot of the rules of hip-hop like a lot of your aggression and","t0":0,"t1":200,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"15":0.05069917776196637,"16":0.10139835552393274,"um":0.08197858795078102,"establis