5888,"familial":0.05471774339159344,"connection":0.04260005557649896,"way":0.018780955567156952,"interact":0.05024546536353235,"another":0.031781514030493195,"although":0.04707233360456003,"had":0.018780955567156952,"lighter":0.05024546536353235,"mood":0.04461106139718715,"compared":0.05024546536353235,"somewhat":0.04260005557649896,"melancholic":0.05471774339159344,"referred":0.05024546536353235,"saltimbank":0.05471774339159344,"symbolized":0.05471774339159344,"artists":0.024847963795059266,"traveling":0.05471774339159344,"town":0.06990929157893108,"always":0.052020179466686814,"fringe":0.04707233360456003,"society":0.039426923817526605,"putting":0.039426923817526605,"performance":0.04260005557649896,"classical":0.04089977438185122,"surface":0.04260005557649896,"while":0.03249337358209268,"painted":0.034071775141223774,"distinctive":0.04707233360456003,"style":0.03591438082392441,"interested":0.04707233360456003,"form":0.03249337358209268,"particularly":0.04461106139718715,"body":0.03812777754843789,"simplified":0.05024546536353235,"fascination":0.04707233360456003,"later":0.031112824767996098,"evolve":0.05471774339159344,"something":0.01702023589369907}},{"id":"doc_1785212350168_21tbmo_c2","text":"would change the art world forever Fernand Olivier played a key role in Picasso's artistic development during the rose period as his lover and Muse Fernand inspired many of his paintings her influence brought new life and energy to his work although their relationship was filled with turbulence it ignited Picasso's exploration of warmth color and affection but as always with Picasso the rose period was just a stepping stone his mind was constantly moving forward searching for something new something unseen something that would soon shake the very foundations of art itself 1907 was a year that would go down in art history this was the year that Pablo Picasso alongside his friend and fellow artist George Brock revolutionized art with a style that had never been seen before cubism cubism was an entirely new way of looking at the world instead of portraying subjects from a single Viewpoint Picasso and Brock would disassemble objects and rearrange them into geometric shapes showing them from multiple angles all at once it was as if they were attempting to show the essence of their subjects revealing not just what they looked like but what they felt like one of the most significant works of this new movement was Picasso's Le demoiselle davino at first glance the painting looks like a jumble of distorted faces and sharp lines but upon closer examination you can see the influence of African art and traditional Iberian sculptures the women's faces are reduced to masks and their bodies appear fragmented as if Picasso was trying to capture the multiple facets of their being cubism wasn't just about breaking objects apart it was about challenging perception it forced people to rethink how they saw the world and question the very nature of reality Picasso's relationship with George Brock was crucial in the development of cubism the two worked side by side often in isolation pushing each other to new heights they would show each other their latest Works challenge each other's techniques and in doing so shatter the boundaries of traditional art though cubism wasn't immediately accepted by the mainstream art World it slowly began to gain recognition and while many critics were left baffled by Picasso's work others saw it for what it was a complete and utter breakthrough in how we understand art Picasso had taken the world of Art and turned it upside down with the creation of cubism Picasso became not just an artist but a cultural Force the world of art would never be the same his influence rippled through the art World inspiring countless movements and genres that would follow Picaso was relentless in his pursuit of innovation even after the Revolutionary impact of cubism he didn't stop instead he continued to experiment with different styles from neoclassicism to surrealism he embraced every influence from Ancient Greek art to African sculptures to Modern industrial design one of the defining traits of Picasso was his fearlessness he never shied away from the","t0":400,"t1":600,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"1907":0.057119981191076113,"change":0.0448212615921682,"art":0.29196875608576955,"world":0.14139549090111833,"forever":0.0448212615921682,"fernand":0.12440830036034992,"olivier":0.048428536897072744,"played":0.057119981191076113,"key":0.057119981191076113,"role":0.05351270588617159,"picasso":0.31577660237068284,"artistic":0.0448212615921682,"development":0.10142937156556686,"during":0.034652923613970515,"rose":0.09685707379414549,"period":0.07387814499936243,"his":0.17080432170104567,"lover":0.057119981191076113,"muse":0.05071468578278343,"inspired":0.036939072499681216,"many":0.04689049736774313,"paintings":0.03397496912611912,"her":0.034652923613970515,"influence":0.15121672504646036,"brought":0.0448212615921682,"life":0.022618194893729952,"energy":0.042023241488780054,"work":0.038290057853780715,"although":0.05351270588617159,"relationship":0.0654400245450325,"filled":0.05071468578278343,"turbulence":0.06220415018017496,"ignited":0.057119981191076113,"exploration":0.057119981191076113,"warmth":0.05351270588617159,"color":0.03973709260306936,"affection":0.06220415018017496,"always":0.02956875462487168,"just":0.061949177538206245,"stepping":0.046495625555618474,"stone":0.05351270588617159,"mind":0.036939072499681216,"constantly":0.040828137271878326,"moving":0.03973709260306936,"forward":0.0448212615921682,"searching":0.05071468578278343,"something":0.05804676384715379,"unseen":0.05351270588617159,"soon":0.040828137271878326,"shake":0.05351270588617159,"very":0.04577572685726494,"foundations":0.06220415018017496,"itself":0.040828137271878326,"year":0.06930584722794103,"go":0.02433072967381413,"down":0.0654400245450325,"history":0.03973709260306936,"pablo":0.035369638885642235,"alongside":0.039737092603