ary":0.06220415018017496,"impact":0.03780418126161509,"he":0.0677059058024101,"didn":0.03004198450663409,"stop":0.03873342880063747,"continued":0.03873342880063747,"experiment":0.046495625555618474,"different":0.02631471686422357,"styles":0.048428536897072744,"neoclassicism":0.057119981191076113,"surrealism":0.048428536897072744,"embraced":0.057119981191076113,"every":0.02911273696761172,"ancient":0.057119981191076113,"greek":0.057119981191076113,"modern":0.03612981729816483,"industrial":0.057119981191076113,"design":0.040828137271878326,"defining":0.06220415018017496,"traits":0.057119981191076113,"fearlessness":0.057119981191076113,"shied":0.06220415018017496,"away":0.03157930772263584}},{"id":"doc_1785212350168_21tbmo_c3","text":"uncomfortable or the unknown for Picasso art was not about perfection it was about exploration about discovery about constantly pushing the boundaries of what was possible Picasso was known to say Every Act of Creation is first an act of Destruction he believed that in order to create something new you had to be willing to destroy what came before this philosophy permeated every aspect of his work whether it was cubism surrealism or classical art Picasso always sought to dismantle the traditional forms in order to rebuild them in his own Vision as Picasso continued to experiment and create he became a living legend galleries around the world scrambled to exhibit his works and his name became synonymous with modern art itself Picasso was not just an artist of the canvas he was an artist of the world and nowhere was this more evident than in his political art as the Spanish Civil War raged in the 1930s Picasso watched from afar with horror in 1937 the town of gernika in Northern Spain was bombed by Nazi planes in support of Franco's nationalist forces the destruction was devastating Picasso was in infuriated by the senseless violence and felt compelled to respond his response was one of the most iconic and Powerful works of art in history gica gica is a massive monochromatic painting that depicts the horrors of war the chaotic composition features distorted figures crying women and slain animals all tangled in a scene of chaos and Devastation the mural sized painting is a raw depiction of the trauma inflicted by War a scream on canvas against the inhumanity of conflict gernika became a symbol of resistance a cultural statement against tyranny it solidified Picasso's role not only as an artist but as a political figure someone who used his art to fight for justice and Humanity throughout his life Picasso remained a fierce opponent of Francisco Franco and his dictatorship in Spain despite receiving multiple invitations to return to his homeland Picasso refused to do so until Spain was was liberated from Franco's rule his opposition to Fascism was not just in his art but in his life gnah was exhibited worldwide bringing attention to the horrors of war and helping to cement Picasso's place not just as an artist but as a voice for peace the painting's raw emotion and unflinching honesty have made it one of the most revered pieces of Art in modern history as Picasso entered the final Decades of his life he remained as prolific as ever he continued to create often working on multiple paintings sculptures and prints at the same time his passion for art never faded if anything it grew stronger as he aged his later works are filled with bold colors simplified shapes and a sense of Freedom that comes with a lifetime of experience he once said it took me 4 years to paint like Raphael but a lifetime to paint like a child Picasso's later works","t0":600,"t1":800,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"1937":0.052152628165349886,"uncomfortable":0.052152628165349886,"unknown":0.052152628165349886,"picasso":0.33339673401307907,"art":0.21341106359530576,"perfection":0.06062317090802484,"exploration":0.05566822104280425,"discovery":0.06062317090802484,"constantly":0.03979045025967136,"pushing":0.049425722477538556,"boundaries":0