"yellow":0.04632880178494309,"costume":0.04254218213569363,"studying":0.039855532738497866,"fine":0.033382263692052626,"arts":0.033382263692052626,"la":0.037771605566682416,"coru":0.039855532738497866,"where":0.03253574251106254,"jos":0.039855532738497866,"taught":0.03040823921616958,"family":0.08072698393682219,"transferred":0.04254218213569363,"there":0.012114971203569152,"following":0.03462930711204717,"closure":0.04632880178494309,"municipal":0.04632880178494309,"museum":0.03462930711204717,"worked":0.06925861422409434,"curator":0.04254218213569363,"refining":0.04254218213569363,"skills":0.037771605566682416,"created":0.026342824142627418,"illustrated":0.04254218213569363,"magazine":0.04632880178494309,"azul":0.04632880178494309,"blanco":0.04632880178494309,"friends":0.027511716870987725,"sadly":0.09265760356988618,"sister":0.07971106547699573,"conchita":0.04254218213569363,"passed":0.031298336520237176,"away":0.023519837241567212,"due":0.037771605566682416,"diphtheria":0.04632880178494309,"age":0.02815603806560194,"seven":0.033382263692052626,"traumatic":0.04632880178494309,"heartbreaking":0.04254218213569363,"expected":0.04254218213569363,"serum":0.04632880178494309,"hurried":0.04632880178494309,"paris":0.026342824142627418,"himself":0.02959564404280317,"vow":0.04254218213569363,"never":0.018351798427346716,"again":0.0253040931470709,"spared":0.04632880178494309}},{"id":"doc_1785212350223_0j82g0_c1","text":"was not to be. Conchita died on January 10th, 1895 and Picasso's vow was broken. Something which he would secretly carry within his art. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; In 1896, the 15-year-old Picasso shared his first studio with Manuel Pallarès, a fellow painter. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Picasso revealed his first large-scale oil painting at the third exhibition [music] of fine arts and industry in Barcelona. The First Communion was a stunning [music] depiction of a Catholic girl kneeling before an altar. Picasso's father, Pepe, modeled [music] for the male figure in the artwork granting a new dimension to the piece. Though the young artist had [music] little interest in religious devotion, his work contained many iconographic scenes. Picasso was inspired by the artists [music] of old to fuel his creative growth. This image marked Picasso's symbolic transition from his [music] youth to adulthood. Picasso's work was now in the public domain &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and the young talented artist had made a huge statement. &gt;&gt; [music] [music] &gt;&gt; In 1897, Picasso was admitted [music] into the Royal Academy of Fine Arts in Madrid. On return to Barcelona, he produced his [music] next wonder, Science and Charity. Picasso was making [music] a huge statement during the realism artistic movement, capturing a window of everyday life. In 1899, Picasso attended [music] a cafe named Els Quatre Gats to meet a circle of avant-garde artists. Picasso was introduced to Carles [music] Casagemas, a Spanish painter and poet. The two became incredibly [music] close friends. Pablo had even painted portraits of Casagemas on their travels throughout Spain. In the art world, there was one place everyone [music] wanted their work to be featured, the bustling French capital, Paris. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; With little knowledge of the language or bustling climate [music] of this new terrain, the painter keenly leapt in. In 1900, Picasso visited Paris with [music] Carles. The two settled in a vacant studio whilst attending exhibitions such as the Universal, which raked in over 32 million visitors, all pining to see [music] the next influx of creative talent. During this time, both Picasso and Carles became infatuated with the women who resided in the city of love. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Picasso met a woman named Berthe Weill, the first and only dealer to promote avant-garde artists. Picasso formed a working [music] relationship with Weill, and she accepted to deal his work. Carles, on the other hand, met a woman named Laure Gargallo, known as Germaine. Carles immediately fell in love, but it was not to be. Germaine had spurned his affections, and in doing so caused Carles to reach a terrible depression. His mood swings [music] became frequent to the degree in which he was a danger to Germaine herself. On the 17th of February, 1901, in a Paris cafe, Carles put a gun to his temple and ended his own life in attendance of his friends. Picasso, who had returned to Barcelona, was heartstruck from the news of his friend's death. Though","t0":200,"t1":400,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"15":0.03073585961969024,"32":0.034006967796349735,"1895":0.03913927050168849,"1896":0.04549620543218809,"1897":0.04549620543218809,"1899":0.041777636878321374,"1900":0.03709279455018983,"1901":0.034006967796349735,"conchita":0.041777636878321374,"died":0.034006967796349735,"january":0.04549620543218809,"10th":0.041777636878321374,"picasso":0.30794595174163025,"vow":0.041777636878321374,"broken":0.035420701947821785,"something":0.014151829017916015,"he":0.03714014809023826,"secretly":0.04549620543218809,"carry":0.041777636878321374,"within":0.0327823355711889,"his":0.21862142398921047,"art":0.03559106176512574,"gt":0.3507440724614029,"music":0.46842460734780866,"year":0.02534519846345548,"old":0.04258619587070111,"shared":0.041777636878321374,"first":0.08143854713854862,"studio":0.054034582131647066,"manuel":0.04549620543218809,"pallar":0.04549620543218809,"fellow":0.03073585961969024,"painter":0.10202090338904919,"revealed":0.041777636878321374,"large":0.035420701947821785,"scale":0.03709279455018983,"oil":0.0327823355711889,"painting":0.022332448737691992,"third":0.03913927050168849,"exhibition":0.03170213339395507,"fine":0.0655646711423778,"arts":0.0655646711423778,"industry":0.03709279455018983,"barcelona":0.10626210584346535,"communion":0.04549620543218809,"stunning":0.04549620543218809,"depiction":0.03709279455018983,"catholic":0.04549620543218809,"girl":0.03709279455018983,"kneeling":0.04549620543218809,"before":0.0