ion":0.05057454609860474,"well":0.019339398296843353,"broader":0.05507611513890492,"social":0.03347216277154979,"political":0.04287906299541348,"upheaval":0.05507611513890492,"europe":0.05057454609860474,"destitute":0.05507611513890492,"commentary":0.05507611513890492,"plight":0.05507611513890492,"poor":0.04287906299541348,"often":0.03429492691676356,"overlooked":0.05507611513890492,"forgotten":0.05507611513890492,"society":0.03968514893252238,"while":0.032706187677573974,"unmistakably":0.05507611513890492,"modern":0.03198966582933112,"shows":0.04287906299541348,"influence":0.03347216277154979,"earlier":0.04287906299541348,"masters":0.04287906299541348}},{"id":"doc_1785212350223_0j82g0_c3","text":"El Greco, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; particularly in the elongated forms and expressive use of color. This fusion of traditional and avant-garde elements is characteristic [music] of Picasso's eclectic approach to art. The painting's [music] emotive power lies in its ability to evoke empathy and introspection in the viewer. The old guitarist's world-weary expression and the stark [music] simplicity of the composition invite contemplation of the human condition and the universal themes of suffering, resilience, [music] and the redemptive power of art. Another of Picasso's art dealers, Pedro Mañach, was appalled by the Blue Period and refused to collaborate. Picasso was now experiencing extreme financial difficulty. He lived a rough bohemian lifestyle &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and now faced complete poverty. His last remaining option was to return to his family. He did in 1903, completely ashamed and unable [music] to quantify his emotions. Though he didn't realize it at the time, the pain Picasso endured during his Blue Period would be the flame that ignited [music] Picasso's adaptiveness. Many could, with time, paint what Picasso could in his youth. However, [music] the ability to embed emotion into art is what truly separated the artist from his peers. This was [music] just the beginning of a great legacy. It took time for Picasso to come to terms with his sorrows. However, with the support of his father, Pablo returned to Paris to meet [music] with some fellow artists with the hopes that his style and financial circumstances might change for the better. His desire was to expand and fundamentally break the [music] rules of what had been traditionally depicted. It was in the Bateau Lavoir studio in Montmartre, where Picasso [music] met Andre Salmon, Guillaume Apollinaire, and Fernande Olivier. Olivier was said to be [music] Picasso's first true love. She modeled for the artist, and soon warmth returned to the previously blue paintings. Picasso painted new subjects from circus performers, harlequins, and many other diverse characters. This would be known as Picasso's Rose Period. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; One of the greatest collections of fantastic art ever assembled goes on display. Dadaism, surrealism, and rayographs. Some are priced above $2,000. &gt;&gt; The whole surrealism that he got &gt;&gt; This is [music] very much part of his work, and it's part of his work because he made it made it his own, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and he created within that particular expression. His sense of humor and [music] wickedness. I mean, they're quite voluptuous, to say the least. One of the very provocative one of the pieces. But, they are unique to him. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Both in paint and spirit, Picasso was optimistic. In 1905, Picasso visited the Van Gogh and Seurat retrospective [music] at the Salon of Independents and the Ingres retrospective at the Salon d'Automne. It was here that Picasso met Gertrude and Leo Stein. Gertrude was a prolific novelist and poet, [music] having close relations with many artists and authors, such as Ernest Hemingway. Picasso wanted to cultivate a healthy","t0":600,"t1":800,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"1903":0.051267939755908036,"1905":0.051267939755908036,"el":0.0399142389199812,"greco":0.0512679397