,"visited":0.036941164414837295,"van":0.04179845633965759,"gogh":0.044104552085372666,"seurat":0.051267939755908036,"retrospective":0.10253587951181607,"salon":0.08820910417074533,"independents":0.051267939755908036,"ingres":0.047077626590516564,"automne":0.051267939755908036,"here":0.021113993416867996,"gertrude":0.0798284778399624,"leo":0.051267939755908036,"stein":0.044104552085372666,"prolific":0.0399142389199812,"novelist":0.051267939755908036,"poet":0.047077626590516564,"having":0.03275085124944582,"close":0.03463506866912222,"relations":0.047077626590516564,"authors":0.047077626590516564,"ernest":0.051267939755908036,"hemingway":0.051267939755908036,"wanted":0.031923643173952125,"cultivate":0.051267939755908036,"healthy":0.051267939755908036}},{"id":"doc_1785212350223_0j82g0_c4","text":"relationship with the wealthy Steins, and so he chose to offer an additional painting to their growing collection at [music] their own salon. &gt;&gt; Gertrude accepted and the portrait had begun. Gertrude Stein took Picasso's [music] breath away. She was homosexual and dressed in the masculine fashion of the day. Her face proved to be a task Picasso had a great deal of trouble with. He could not fully encapsulate Gertrude's form in the ways he had done before. Gertrude sat for hours in a broken arm chair in her home number 27 Rue de Fleurus. Picasso faced her on a kitchen stool, easel set. After some time of relentless painting, he left her portrait unfinished and traveled to Spain with Fernande Olivier. On the 11th of May, 1906, [music] they ventured to Gósol, a small village northwest of Comarca Berguedà in Catalonia. He eventually came across [music] a statue of the Virgin and Child of Gósol. Its archaic minimalism served as the core inspiration [music] for Gertrude's mask-like face alongside ethnographic motifs [music] inspired by Iberian sculptures Picasso had observed at the Louvre. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; At first, Gertrude was confused. The enigmatic portrait [music] didn't traditionally represent her likeness. But she was accepting once Picasso had explained the style was connotative of her ambitions to become, in his words, the high priestess [music] of modern art. &gt;&gt; 4000 BC, Egypt. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; 1928, Picasso. When we look beyond formal similarities, however, we can feel a difference. Sometimes these [music] ancient examples of modern-looking art were meant simply as decoration. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; In 1907, Picasso used his new-found funding to build [music] his own collection. He had acquired two Iberian statues. The purchase of these statues [music] were thanks to his new-found fascination with African art, leading to a short movement within Cubism. He merged both styles as a homage [music] to traditional Egyptian and Iberian paintings and sculptures. He didn't know at the time that such statues were stolen from the Louvre. Such purchase drew suspicions [music] to Picasso for the theft on the Mona Lisa in 1911. The painter aptly returned the statues anonymously to avoid a conspiracy. [music] &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; The new-found style depicted Gertrude's celebration of innovation as she had also done with Henri Matisse. It is said by many that Picasso's work for Gertrude Stein was the foundation for his future project, Les Demoiselles [music] d'Avignon, his first artwork cast in bronze. Perhaps one of his most known works during this period, a beautiful piece depicting five nude Spanish prostitutes in a brothel. Similarly to Stein's portrait, the work's faces adopted a geometric appearance. Picasso, alongside Georges Braque, a famed French painter, had co-founded the Cubism movement, alongside revolutionizing collage, which Picasso would also eventually adopt. Braque learned from his father, who was a house painter. He learned to imitate marbled surfaces and wood grain. Picasso would be inspired by this and utilize such techniques in his own work. &gt;&gt; Half","t0":800,"t1":1000,"tsReal":false