 &gt;&gt; Just off 5th Avenue, on a site long occupied by the old-fashioned town dwellings of the Rockefeller family, stands the spanking new home of a nationally important institution, the New York Museum of Modern Art. No dusty storehouse for old masters, this museum is an up-to-the-minute showroom for art in our time. Painting and sculpture, architecture and still photography. &gt;&gt; So, if we start off all of those years you were collecting, [music] and then I think maybe 10, 12 years in, you met him, didn't you? You told me that story [music] and the significance of the story. &gt;&gt; Well, I think we went started initially in in the '50s, '54, something like this, but in 1963, uh &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; we went as usual during the summer holidays with the children, and so on. We walked into the [music] portrait of the gallery, and it was full of people, which was very unusual. And I realized that he was there [music] because it was his private show of his most recent work. And Madame Ramie, who owned the gallery, [music] brought him over to meet us. Well, I mean, if you would say this is Beethoven [music] or Shakespeare, or whatever, I wouldn't have been at all surprised. I I was absolutely mesmerized. [music] He was much smaller than I thought he'd be, quite dumpy, but but small. And he beamed [music] all the time, and the great humor in his ceramic, and so on, was very much part of his persona, it seemed to me. And he took [music] something out of his white coat, which was a little medallion like this, and with a with a ribbon, he pinned it round Sheila's neck, [music] and said that he much preferred to give presents to women than he did to men. And I accepted that fact. [music] But Madame Ramie had sort of tuned him","t0":1000,"t1":1200,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"10":0.029314764117185452,"12":0.039198138176188294,"54":0.048689981804405286,"1901":0.044639360913797506,"1963":0.054839555878814436,"million":0.03200107767349616,"pounds":0.05483955587881