d":0.03365165147088695,"old":0.02652182665945742,"transform":0.05203654474763274,"own":0.023651017598011238,"voice":0.044118602190657205}},{"id":"doc_1785212350223_0j82g0_c7","text":"to the Newcastle art story, which brings modern and impressionist paintings from Swiss private collections and museums to Paris. Masterpieces by Dufy, Lagar, Vallotton, as well as Picasso, Manet, Van Gogh, Cezanne, and Gauguin are included. Some 200 paintings and 50 sculptures are borrowed by the French government. Recent art frauds and record-breaking high prices for master works have kept all kinds of art in the world news spotlight. The show is a hit in the most critical art city [music] on the globe. &gt;&gt; Picasso remained close with Fernande Olivier. In 1909, the two temporarily [music] settled in a Paris studio, and together they visited Horta, a neighborhood in Barcelona. Picasso had [music] visited before when he was sick. Now rejuvenated, he sought to return for both artistic inspiration [music] and family ties to the city. Picasso developed upon [music] his craft by attending countless exhibitions across the globe. From Berlin to New York, his name was now [music] prevalent amongst the international market. &gt;&gt; Why is it such a [music] a splendid piece of Why is it got such a broad valuation? &gt;&gt; Well, it's a very powerful and expressive work by Picasso. He [snorts] painted it in 1955. The subject is Jacqueline. And there's a lot of [music] of of influences on Picasso at this time. Um he's looking particularly at Delacroix at Les Femmes d'Alger. Um he's looking at Ingres. Um two artists [music] from from uh the the Old Master period who uh provided endless source of fascination [music] for Picasso throughout his life. Uh but he's also looking at Matisse as well. Picasso and Matisse were his great &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; um sparring partners of the 20th century. Uh Matisse had just died. Um uh and Picasso felt that [music] that it it left a hole in terms of of competition and and and art historical discussion for him. Um so, this picture is very much about uh Jacqueline. It's very much a a paean to uh to Jacqueline, um his last uh and and longest-lasting muse of his life, um who he would marry about 5 years after this picture was painted. Um but it's also a a tribute to some of the great masters of the past uh and also a tribute to his relationship with Matisse. Well, Jacqueline was an incredibly uh powerful and and strong character. Uh I think you you had to be to uh uh to be with Picasso for that length of time. &gt;&gt; Alongside his artistic talents, the painter was also multilingual and had a profound ability to communicate with a multitude of cultures. It was apparent [music] that Picasso was attempting to strip away the bonds which kept him and many artists [music] tied to tradition. He wanted to unlearn, to seek his own interpretation. Audiences saw this [music] convention-breaking nature quickly in Picasso's sculpture work, since he wasn't taught [music] the form in an academic sense. The artist remained in Paris, moving [music] studio","t0":1400,"t1":1600,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"50":0.03945679489848033,"200":0.05475919927970735,"1909":0.05028353291276287,"1955":0.047107997089093835,"newcastle":0.05475919927970735,"art":0.10709343918130589,"story":0.02732992634092235,"brings":0.04464486014427161,"modern":0.03180559270786682,"impressionist":0.05475919927970735,"paintings":0.05981729834778779,"swiss":0.05028353291276287,"private":0.0381566643552049,"collections":0.05028353291276287,"museums":0.05475919927970735,"paris":0.09340919047588482,"masterpieces":0.05028353291276287,"dufy":0.05475919927970735,"lagar":0.05475919927970735,"vallotton":0.05475919927970735,"well":0.07691246651043547,"picasso":0.27798264017753826,"manet":0.05475919927970735,"van":0.04464486014427161,"gogh":0.047107997089093835,"cezanne":0.05475919927970735,"gauguin":0.05028353291276287,"included":0.05475919927970735,"some":0.03716984788141395