l":0.04259093033579003,"ingl":0.047062250369478295,"because":0.017198869096257376,"passion":0.03590668770321142,"english":0.044601505384453816,"told":0.03248641326592949,"biographer":0.047062250369478295,"roland":0.047062250369478295,"penrose":0.047062250369478295,"way":0.0187769325503328,"london":0.04259093033579003,"stopped":0.03941847828464223,"off":0.027772717935415414,"paris":0.031106160175396386,"never":0.021670189129945644,"carried":0.05470602245431435,"finished":0.047062250369478295,"journey":0.040891013353699386,"reason":0.033247241268863324,"partly":0.05470602245431435,"admiration":0.05470602245431435,"british":0.10046940484125215,"painting":0.026853216229757233,"examines":0.05470602245431435,"later":0.031106160175396386,"kind":0.026004518183577435,"shows":0.04259093033579003,"much":0.025603469184027273,"really":0.018566876064398764,"looking":0.028262915618375357,"renaissance":0.05470602245431435,"classical":0.040891013353699386,"tradition":0.047062250369478295,"then":0.0206191435335393,"distorting":0.05470602245431435,"abstracting":0.05470602245431435,"there":0.014305612516644537,"dozen":0.044601505384453816,"including":0.03811961030210176,"most":0.01965961408128185,"important":0.028262915618375357,"uh":0.042795776166386475,"portrait":0.03590668770321142,"serge":0.044601505384453816,"lifar":0.044601505384453816,"executed":0.044601505384453816,"estimated":0.04259093033579003,"francs":0.044601505384453816}},{"id":"doc_1785212350223_0j82g0_c9","text":"&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; From 1916 to 1923, Picasso collaborated with the Ballets Russes, a renowned ballet company founded by Sergei Diaghilev. One of his most notable collaborations with the company was for the ballet Parade, which premiered in 1917. Picasso was responsible for the costume and set design for Parade, which was composed by Erik Satie, with choreography by Léonide Massine. This collaboration marked an important intersection of visual art, music, and dance, showcasing Picasso's innovative approach to stage design, and his influence on avant-garde performance art. During his work with the company, Picasso met Olga Khokhlova, a Russian ballet dancer. The two formed [music] a strong relationship and became husband and wife just a year later. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; This office in central London was the studio where Picasso worked on his designs for the Ballets Russes. And although collaborating with visual artists was part of the philosophy behind the company, Picasso was by far the highest profile artist to become involved. And exploring the possibilities of working in other art forms could be one of his most important areas of influence. &gt;&gt; I think before it was it was very separate. Um it you had specialist in art, you had [music] specialist in music, you had specialist in dance. Um and it it only improves [music] when all three of those come together. And it when you have people composing for ballet, for example. Before before it wasn't, there was music and we danced [music] to it. Um and I I think these these things only make the art form, let's call it the whole art form, even better. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Picasso remained in Paris during World War I. Being a foreign national, he was exempt from conscription. He and many other artists used [music] the climate to fuel their work. Picasso formed relationships with figures such as Guillaume Apollinaire, Jean Cocteau, and Sergei Diaghilev, who were influential in the cultural milieu of Paris during the war years. Picasso continued his efforts in portraiture, working with Marie-Thérèse Walter, a Parisian model. In 1930, Picasso, now 48 years old, bought a private estate. He established [music] a sculpture workshop in the Château de Boisgeloup. During his time at Boisgeloup, Picasso explored various artistic endeavors, including painting, drawing, sculpture, and ceramics. The tranquil surroundings of the estate provided him with the solitude [music] and inspiration he needed to produce a significant body of work. The painter seemed to desire constant innovation, teetering between various styles. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Picasso may have chronicled the struggles of the early part of the 20th century, but his works and message of peace is no less pertinent [music] today. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Neoclassicism became prevalent once more in his projects. It was significantly separate from his Cubism style due to the stricter line work and focus on precise human anatomy. He took a great deal of interest in Greek mythology, often depicting the Minotaur. Many believe the image of the Minotaur was representative","t0":1800,"t1":2000,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"48":0.039473079595325915,"1916":0.05280904046090303,"1917":0.05280904046090303,"1923":0.05280904046090303,"1930":0.05280904046090303,"gt":0.3731941948398341,"music":0.3479787915648802,"picasso":0.2680827456511838,"collaborated":0.05280904046090303,"ballets":0.10561808092180607,"russes":0.10561808092180607,"renowned":0.05280904046090303,"ballet":0.21123616184361213,"company":0.13153303776662045,"founded":0.048492767590399936,"sergei":0.10561808092180607,"diaghilev":0.10561808092180607,"his":0.18125825024297135,"most":0.03795579751136796,"notable":0.03567618966984243,"collaborations":0.05280904046090303,"parade":0.10561808092180607,"premiered":0.05280904046090303,"responsible":0.05280904046090303,"costume":0.048492767590399936,"design":0.06932318010578957,"composed":0.05280904046090303,"erik":0.05280904046090303,"satie":0.05280904046090303,"choreography":0.05280904046090303,"onide":0.05280904046090303,"massine":0.05280904046090303,"collaboration":0.048492767590399936,"marked":0.03805160604354505,"important":0.05456574568843159,"intersection":0.048492767590399936,"visual":0.0761032120870901,"art":0.12393537898028373,"dance":0.08610981375704282,"showcasing":0.048492767590399936,"innovative":0.048492767590399936,"approach":0.039473079595325915,"stage":0.03679777751121786,"influence":0.06418872477329385,"avant"