3308451893,"developing":0.05536870075708514,"unique":0.043106853156754034,"free":0.03740541870752141,"standing":0.047632336272277476,"sculpture":0.0827726886532727,"woman":0.03740541870752141,"outstretched":0.05536870075708514,"arms":0.045141783192329074,"playful":0.05536870075708514,"addition":0.05536870075708514,"works":0.055268248374277386,"had":0.03800877171735803,"developed":0.045141783192329074,"since":0.045141783192329074,"art":0.021657090187855325,"piece":0.035370488671946365,"inspired":0.032879935591997984,"childhood":0.09028356638465815,"cut":0.045141783192329074,"pieces":0.0827726886532727,"paper":0.045141783192329074,"shape":0.047632336272277476,"animals":0.0827726886532727,"sisters":0.05536870075708514,"enjoy":0.04138634432663635,"reference":0.043106853156754034,"adored":0.05536870075708514,"were":0.02193268973790607,"built":0.043106853156754034,"sheet":0.05536870075708514,"metal":0.050843217641561696,"acquired":0.047632336272277476,"scrap":0.05536870075708514,"shop":0.05536870075708514,"far":0.03740541870752141,"home":0.032879935591997984,"southern":0.05536870075708514,"france":0.047632336272277476,"figures":0.03989597178746979,"created":0.03148296288387263,"most":0.01989775970233102,"featured":0.043106853156754034,"nude":0.047632336272277476,"females":0.050843217641561696,"some":0.018791784865406665,"owls":0.050843217641561696,"monkeys":0.050843217641561696,"pablo":0.03148296288387263,"produced":0.045141783192329074,"around":0.02477759685545059,"paintings":0.030241549692850492,"impressive":0.050843217641561696,"career":0.045141783192329074,"depicted":0.04138634432663635,"artist":0.02342306227707263,"emotions":0.050843217641561696,"time":0.018378359280282783,"creation":0.04138634432663635,"encapsulating":0.05536870075708514,"feelings":0.047632336272277476,"sociopolitical":0.05536870075708514,"conflict":0.047632336272277476,"times":0.03634164132984651,"capturing":0.045141783192329074,"loneliness":0.05536870075708514,"depression":0.045141783192329074,"instances":0.05536870075708514,"joy":0.043106853156754034,"spurs":0.05536870075708514,"imagination":0.045141783192329074,"last":0.029669054222713744,"aptly":0.047632336272277476,"named":0.03740541870752141,"self":0.032879935591997984,"portrait":0.03634164132984651}},{"id":"doc_1785212350223_0j82g0_c13","text":"facing death. His means of creating work in his later years was somewhat minimalist. It wasn't a step backwards, however. The artist had become innately aware of what he needed to do to tell a story without complex requirements. It was as both a testament to his childhood works and an amalgamation of everything he had explored in his vast career. The picture showed Picasso facing his death head-on. &gt;&gt; This self-portrait [music] in pencil was drawn when he was 91. It's like a death mask. Picasso, for so long defiant of death, finally seems to acknowledge that he, too, is terrifyingly mortal. The show of late Picasso at the Tate offers a careful [music] selection from the last 20 years of his life, from 1953 to '73. The bulk of it is work by a man in his 80s. In his dotage, Picasso basked in a celebrity usually reserved for film stars. \"If I spit,\" he said, \"they'll take it, frame it, and sell it as great art.\" &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Picasso died at the Mas Notre Dame de Vie in Mougins on the 8th of April, 1978. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; I do think that artists in the 20th and 21st century have wanted to do a great deal more. Picasso [music] himself was never stuck doing just one thing. He didn't just do paintings, so it's basically always constructions, going into ceramics. You name it, he did it. And I think there is that sort of sense of artists [music] seizing opportunities. There are a great deal more opportunities to to work on. &gt;&gt; The exhibition [music] exploring Picasso's relationship with British artists opens at Tate Britain tomorrow. It travels to the Scottish National Gallery of Modern Art in Edinburgh [music] in August. &gt;&gt; The work of Pablo Picasso is a testament to all, whether it be aspiring artists or those [music] who wish to look inwards at their turbulent human nature. Paintings are only as powerful as those who create [music] them. And Picasso, who had lived through such remarkable events, used such experience to remain alive in his body of work. Work which remains prevalent &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; to this day. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Mhm.","t0":2600,"t1":2748,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"20":0.03873352782146849,"73":0.0625524729777328,"91":0.05860212169385431,"1953":0.0625524729777328,"1978":0.05553799118717821,"facing":0.11720424338770861,"death":0.1488248626043243,"his":0.210430072922942,"means":0.04241723662831562,"creating":0.0395660042791989,"work":0.12579509442776066,"later":0.03873352782146849,"years":0.05610871228273497,"somewhat":0.05303441427040511,"minimalist":0.06812018040118199,"wasn":0.04241723662831562,"step":0.04746670684695592,"backwards":0.0625524729777328,"however":0.05303441427040511,"artist":0.02881742222672857,"had":0.07014335764098067,"become":0.03583190401728316,"innately":0.06812018040118199,"aware":0.05303441427040511,"he":0.1297540820382785,"needed":0.05091767014806004,"tell":0.02962539973755733,"story":0.03399829685574971,"without":0.05091767014806004,"complex":0.0625524729777328,"requirements":0.06812018040118199,"both":0.04241723662831562,"testament":0.11720424338770861,"childhood":0.05553799118717821,"works":0.03399829685574971,"amalgamation":0.06812018040118199,"everything":0.03289917792098792,"explored":0.0625524729777328,"vast":0.06812018040118199,"career":0.05553799118717821,"picture":0.05091767014806004,"showed":0.05860212169385431,"picasso":0.2593568000405571,"head":0.049084062986526594,"gt":0.4813959059904617,"self":0.040452225056401336,"portrait":0.044711165868334214,"music":0.3366522736964099,"pencil":0.06812018040118199,"drawn":0.0625524729777328,"like":0.01712668589366588,"mask":0.05303441427040511,"long":0.034582710844524925,"defiant":0.06812018040118199,"finally":0.05303441427040511,"seems":0.0625524729777328,"acknowledge":0.0625524729777328,"too":0.03650187377252283,"terrifyingly":0.06812018040118199,"mortal":0.06812018040118199,"show":0.032380940716179044,"late":0.049084062986526594,"tate":0.11720424338770861,"offers":0.0625524729777328,"careful":0.0625524729777328,"selection":0.0625524729777328,"last":0.03650187377252283,"life":0.024769336323173994,"bulk":0.06812018040118199,"man":0.034582710844524925,"80s":0.05303441427040511,"dotage":0.06812018040118199,"basked":0.06812018040118199,"celebrity":0.05553799118717821,"usually":0.05553799118717821,"reserved":0.06812018040118199,"film":0.044711165868334214,"stars":0.06812018040118199,"spit":0.05860212169385431,"said":0.025990732611751992,"ll":0.027331470497538733,"take":0.030483907778621144,"frame":0.05303441427040511,"sell":0.03720621565108107,"great":0.08306447083126013,"art":0.05328948920198715,"died":0.05091767014806004,"mas":0.05860212169385431,"notre":0.05860212169385431,"dame":0.05860212169385431,"de":0.04601993247985052,"vie":0.05860212169385431,"mougins":0.05860212169385431,"8th":0.05303441427040511,"april":0.05303441427040511,"think":0.04329379442086091,"artists":0.12373666539629455,"20th":0.04746670684695592,"21st":0.0625524729777328,"century":0.04601993247985052,"wanted":0.04241723662831562,"deal":0.11107598237435642,"himself":0.04351635556307741,"never":0.026983815065195125,"stuck":0.05860212169385431,"doing":0.029215469114140788,"just":0.03392047907884136,"thing":0.023647761690691597,"didn":0.03289917792098792,"paintings":0.07441243130216214,"basically":0.04746670684695592,"always":0.032380940716179044,"constructions":0.0625524729777328,"going":0.023119555951074008,"ceramics":0.05553799118717821,"name":0.03399829685574971,"there":0.03562682358042208,"sort":0.05303441427040511,"sense":0.0395660042791989,"seizing":0.06812018040118199,"opportunities":0.11107598237435642,"exhibition":0.04746670684695592,"exploring":0.049084062986526594,"relationship":0.03583190401728316,"british":0.0625524729777328,"opens":0.06812018040118199,"britain":0.0625524729777328,"tomorrow":0.06812018040118199,"travels":0.0625524729777328,"scottish":0.06812018040118199,"national":0.05303441427040511,"gallery":0.05091767014806004,"modern":0.0395660042791989,"edinburgh":0.06812018040118199,"august":0.0625524729777328,"pablo":0.03873352782146849,"whether":0.049084062986526594,"aspiring":0.06812018040118199,"wish":0.05091767014806004,"look":0.029215469114140788,"inwards":0.0625524729777328,"turbulent":0.06812018040118199,"human":0.03873352782146849,"nature":0.05860212169385431,"only":0.03289917792098792,"powerful":0.049084062986526594,"create":0.03343774326584675,"lived":0.049084062986526594,"through":0.03139969079780323,"remarkable":0.06812018040118199,"events":0.05860212169385431,"experience":0.044711165868334214,"remain":0.06812018040118199,"alive":0.05860212169385431,"body":0.04746670684695592,"remains":0.0625524729777328,"prevalent":0.05860212169385431,"day":0.03873352782146849,"mhm":0.04139961144073234}}],"kind":"transcript","tsMode":"estimate"},{"id":"doc_1785765215478_14t4u0","name":"futur_dont_chase_virality","text":"Uh about a week ago, I was asked to speak on a topic and I've put together a brand new presentation for some of you. So bear with me if there are some typos and some weird things. And the reason why I did this is because they asked me to talk about the topic of virality. And nobody does a better job than that than Brendan Kaine. He's our friend. Uh we he's been on multiple podcasts with us. So Brendan, with all apologies, I'm going to take a little different approach and talk to you about something because when I think of Mind Valley, I think of a more spiritual personal uh pursuit versus this thing. There's this promise of social media that anyone can go viral. And I wonder why or where we got this idea from that anyone can go viral, right? And I think it's the social media networks have told us this and they make this promise like they dangle that little carrot for us to pursue and it keeps us chasing. So I want to ask you this question. If anyone can go viral. The question is should you? So I want you to think about this. Okay. Some of you may know this person. His name is Mark Manson. Do you know Mark Manson? Anybody? Okay, great. Well read group here. I love that. He wrote this book called The Subtle Art of Not Giving an F. And I heard him say this thing about if you want to be a 1enter, and I don't think he's talking about the top 1% of the richest people in the world, but the people who make the greatest impact in the world, the top 1enter, they have something in common. They all do this one thing. They are contrarians. When everyone's going one direction, they go the opposite direction. But any fool can go a different direction. So you have to be a correct contrarian. You have to find something that goes against what the mainstream is saying, whatever it is that you strongly believe in your heart. Not to do it because it's it's a gimmick, but because you believe in your heart, this isn't correct. Because if you say what everyone else is saying, you're going to disappear in all the noise. So, you have to become a correct contrarian. And I take this very personally because I am not trying to be contrarian, but I have very strong points of view on lots of things. So, I'm going to use this opportunity to probably undo everything that Brendan just said. Okay. So, we're gonna have a little bit of fun together. Okay. So, from the people who are laughing, I presume you were here for that. And for the people who are not laughing, maybe you're going to be mad at me or you're just new here. So, it's okay. This presentation exists with or without Brendan. He's a very important person. So, I want to ask you this question, okay, to reflect on this idea a little bit, which is in an era of fast fashion, what is the consequence of fast fashion? You know, certain brands that make things really quickly, they not only destroy legitimate fashion brands by knocking off their designs, but they're producing things that are such a rapid pace that they are the most harmful companies to the environment, even more than big oil. It's a shocking idea. So if we like fast fashion, we have to come to the consequence and understand what happens to all the clothes that we don't buy because it's become cheaper to replace the clothes than it is to wash the clothes. That's scary to me. And then in the era of fast food, what's the consequence of fast food? We have a lot of engineered things that last a really long time. Good for shelf life, not good for your body. And I don't know about you, I prefer things that are less processed over things that are processed in an era of easy money. What could be the problem with easy money? What could be the problem? Well, you know, get rich quick, you also lose it really quick. Easy come, easy go. Very high percentage of people who win the lottery wind up right back where they started. I wonder why. Because maybe, just maybe, there is something to the pursuit of learning something, working hard that is more desirable than the result itself. So, I hope you start to hear a theme here, and I'm going to build my argument. And then we think about things like a crash diet. Anybody here been on a crash diet? New Year's resolution to lose a lot of weight. The problem with crash diets there, the the hint is in the title itself, crash. Maybe the word die. So, you're on this yo-yo diet, right? When you may start off large and get to medium small, but because this is not a lifelong pursuit because you see it as a very short-term goal to hit, maybe once you hit the goal, you regress because those habits haven't been formed inside of you. Kind of an important thing to think about. Okay? And then, I'm this is not a real term. Does anybody here want fast fans? Okay. Okay. I didn't think so. You don't have to agree with me. We can have an argument. This can be very interactive. So, people of convenience maybe. So, what's the problem with fast fans? Well, I don't know about you. This is a generated image, but it's a symbol for the things that are going on with engagement farms. You pay, you get the views. As soon as you stop paying, you don't get the views. You don't get the fake engagement. And I would rather have a hundred people who are dedicated who truly want to consume my content than a million people who are there because somebody's clicking it in some country. So that's that's it with that. So what I would like for you to maybe kind of rethink this idea of v virality. We came out of a pandemic. I don't know about you, but I'm not so keen on viruses myself. So the antidote probably to this idea of going viral is to be antiviral. So my talk is called the dark side of virality. Why success or easy success might become a prison sentence for many of you. Okay. So I'm going to ask this question and I do a style of talks where I'd like to interact with you as much as you're comfortable doing and I understand different cultures people like to talk some people don't. I will work with whatever you give me. Okay. So what is the goal of content creation? I just want you to reflect on this. So, if you're watching this on Zoom, just type it in. What is the goal of you creating content? I'm going to give you a second because maybe there's a delay. Then I'm going to ask the audience here, what's the goal of you create? How many people here are creating content? You make a post, a podcast, a carousel, uh a YouTube video, Instagram reel. So, almost everybody here, I'm glad to hear that at least the programming is right because this is the social media summit of the 1 billion followers summit. So, if you're here, maybe you're curious or you're actually doing something. So just randomly yell out just like a little bit at a time, not all at once cuz then I can't process that. What's the goal of you creating content &gt;&gt; right here? What's that &gt;&gt; connection? &gt;&gt; Oh yeah, hold on. Hold on everybody. Oh my god, I know where we are now. This is like uh bizarre. We're trading things. Yes. I just need to understand the word. I can manage &gt;&gt; uh sales. &gt;&gt; Sales to sell things. You make content to sell things. Okay, great. &gt;&gt; Educate. &gt;&gt; Educating. Are you an educator? &gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; Okay, that's why. [laughter] Okay. Over here. &gt;&gt; Awareness. &gt;&gt; Awareness. To build awareness so you can get sales or to educate? &gt;&gt; Educate. &gt;&gt; Okay. So, education. Okay. Yes. &gt;&gt; Entertain. &gt;&gt; Entertain. Do you tell jokes? Are you a funny guy? &gt;&gt; Uh, yeah. I I do funny vlogs and stuff. &gt;&gt; Okay. Maybe not right now, but Okay. Over here. &gt;&gt; Making money. &gt;&gt; Oh, we got to make some money, huh? Okay. Last one. &gt;&gt; What? &gt;&gt; Fashion. &gt;&gt; No. Fashion. Fashion. Passion. &gt;&gt; Passion. I love that. What's your passion, friend? &gt;&gt; Uh, history. &gt;&gt; Oh, so education. &gt;&gt; No. &gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. It's okay. Okay. You know. Okay. One more. You're She's like, \"Pick me or I'm going to be mad at you. You're going to get an angry view for me.\" Yes. &gt;&gt; You want to create clones of yourself? &gt;&gt; Online. And then share your knowledge. &gt;&gt; Okay. It might be a language translation thing. I'm ESL too, so maybe there's another word. I think what you're saying is you can make a positive impact in the world and inspire people to do the things that you want to inspire them to do. That's what So you're talking about thought leadership through education. Yes. Beautiful. Okay. Thank you for participating. Okay. So I I took the guess that maybe some of you want to do awareness, build awareness either to promote and to create sales or awareness around an idea, eat less of this, eat more of that, uh be kinder, you know, those kinds of things or or meditate more, whatever the idea is. Some of you want conversion to create content to get more sales, right? And some of you, nobody said this, but does anybody create content to build trust? Yeah. Okay. There's an asterisk on that because we're going to come back to that in a little bit. Okay. I just realized I'm going too slow. I need to speed up. Okay. 10 minutes have been lost. Okay. When we do awareness, it's not just called content creation. It's called marketing. You understand that? Yes. So what I don't like is when we use one term to describe too many things then the words lose meaning and we have to be specific about the words that we use because the words shape our worlds. Yes. Okay. When we do conversion what is that called then? &gt;&gt; It's called sales. Very smart audience. So what I don't want to do is mix all this stuff up. But when we build trust what do we call that? &gt;&gt; Branding. Yes. Good job guys. Great job. Look at this. We agree. So far so good. Okay. So your goals. So, if we're going to create content, what's the goal of creating content? Well, let's let's play along. Let's say we really want to go viral. Does anybody here want to go viral? Raise your hands. &gt;&gt; Yes. Some of you are like less excited about raising your hand now because you're like, \"It's a trap. It's a trap. Don't do it.\" But let's be honest with each other. We can do that. So, let's say your goal is to be viral. Okay. So, what are the two options of going viral? Uh, as far as I can tell, yes or no. Okay. So, what happens if we actually get our wish of going viral? Here's what happens. Awesome. We get great reach. We get more engagement. We get more follows. Hallelujah. Maybe we make some money through monetization. We're really excited about making more content cuz shoot, we're rewarded for it, right? That's how the algorithms sink their hooks into you, by the way. And I went viral. You can just wackle with a little bit more confidence. Yes. Not like that, but you know what I mean. Okay. So, what happens now? Well, we we get into this loop. We went viral. We need to go viral again. There's a little problem here. Then we enter into this trap. It's called the validation loop where we're looking outside to be validated versus looking inwards to be validated, right? Exttrinsic versus intrinsic. That's the problem. So, even at your best possible outcome, you get what you want, but now you're stuck. You want it again and again. I'm going to go deeper into this in a second. So the question for you is, is it sustainable? Now, we need to have a common definition. What does it mean to go viral? It means that you have an outlier. Something that is 10x what you normally produce. You get 100 views, you get a,000 views. That's viral. If you do a,000 views, you get um what is it? 10,000 views. That's viral. So maybe my math is just bad, right? So 10x. How many people here have made a piece of content that's gone viral for them? Raise your hands. Okay, those of you keep your hands up. Keep your hands up. Air out the armpits. Some of you are sweating. Okay, you've gone viral. How many were able to repeat it? Lower your hand if you can't repeat it. And how many have been able to sustain it over and over and over again? Lower your hands now. Only a handful of people. The person in the front, of course, she's must be really smart and very good at making content. Beautiful. Okay. So, let's take the other path now. Okay. What happens when you don't go viral? This is the dark side that nobody is really paying attention to, which is now I'm not really motivated to make content anymore. And my ego, my self-esteem, my self-worth, my self- value takes a hit. And sometimes it leads to depression. And if you do this for long enough, it leads to burnout. Has anybody ever felt burnout on creating content? I mean, everybody's hand should be up right now. Let's tell the truth, people, please. Okay? And then what happens is eventually burnout leads to you quitting. So I presented to you a very structured argument why neither option is good. Yes, maybe we can argue later. So the problem with virality is it's based on an outcome. Do I get to go viral or not? And we have to ask this question. Is there something beneath the surface that's a bigger problem? So I'm going to present to you four really quick problems and we'll go through this. Okay. Number one is the seduction. Okay. Virality feels a lot of times very opaque. None of the platforms tell you how to go viral. So there's all these professional gurus, coaches, and programs that you can enter telling you what they guess what it is even though the platforms do not tell you. It's a very opaque process. And I think what they give they can take away. And I personally do not like to export my emotional well-being to a machine to the corporate overlords. Okay? Maybe you you like it, I don't like it. So I talked about this before. So what we used to look inside for whether or not we're doing the right thing, now we're trusting a machine that we do not understand from our corporate overlords who never talk to us to regulate our emotional well-being. I don't like this at all. And I'm going to share a little story with you. There's my friend, her name is Jodie Cook, and I've talked about her in a couple different ways. Jodie Cook on Instagram has 155 posts and 10.7,000 followers. Her typical content is 2,000 2,000. And wait a minute, there's a viral post right there. It has 2.9 million uh views and then it goes back to 4,229. Okay, now my friend Jodie Cook, who I featured on my channel, so it's a collab reel, has all this attention. She goes, \"Chris, I've never gone viral before. Is this what it feels like for you and your life?\" I'm like, \"No, I don't go viral either. I don't know what's going on.\" Okay, so let's take a look here. That's the clip. All right, look at the engagement. So they're like, \"Finally, I made it. I made it. So, Jod is um she writes for Forbes as a senior contributor. The impact of this one post going viral inspired her or maybe demanded of her to write an article and she wrote, \"What really happens when you go viral on Instagram?\" So, number one is your notifications are blowing up and she's trying to not pay attention to the phone, but it's going off all the time. She can't focus on anything else. She's constantly checking her phone to the point in which she has to turn it off and turn off everything. And now because she wants to keep checking the new numbers and the messages that are coming because it's so addictive, she came and do basic tasks and her inbox is crazy. It's flooded with messages. But there's this other part which maybe some of you have experienced. Threats, harassment, [snorts] name calling, and bullying are coming in. Yeah. Anybody have experienced that before? The trolls. Yes. not nice and if you're not built for it, if you haven't earned it over time, it can be overwhelming. So, I'm just going to read some of the more sanitized comments that were left on this post. Okay, number one, uh I can't read this. All right, it says, \"When all you are is money, how poor your life must be.\" This is a complete stranger. And there's a reason why they say this when you go viral, because they don't know you. They just see this thing and they judge you for everything based on one thing you said or the way that you look or how long your skirt is or if you wear a baseball cap when you talk or not. Okay, here's another one. Uggh. These guru prices are out of control. The coaching space needs serious regulation in place. This person, I guarantee you, has done zero research on looking into her programs and how she coaches. You feeling it? Have you had comments like this before? &gt;&gt; No. I love the fact that you're putting them out there. Is that wrong? [laughter] &gt;&gt; You know what? You know what bullies don't like is being bullied themselves. &gt;&gt; How you like them apples. You know, I could tell you stories, but I'm not going to today &gt;&gt; unless there's time. What's that? &gt;&gt; Tell them. &gt;&gt; I love my bullies. I love the trolls. I'll tell you later. Love the enemy. It's the best approach. I make more content from the bullies than I do of the lovers and the fans. I'll tell you why. Because there's a friction point. The internet loves friction. So, I have an opportunity to clarify what I meant to say or how you might have misinterpreted this. It even usually it outperforms original posts. Okay, here's the last one. One word, escort. Yeah, I told you I'm giving you the more sanitized version. You guys know what escort means, right? Yes. Okay. I don't know. We're in Dubai. I don't know what we can talk about here. I'll probably have canceled. I will never be here again. Okay. Number two, the addiction. the addiction happens. You chase the dragon. Do you know the term chase the dragon? Okay, probably not here, but anyways, there's this flood of uh happiness, dopamine, and then dopamine leads to depression. Okay, and I think this whole thing is very close to a different kind of relationship, a perilous dealer addict relationship. You're the addict, you're not the dealer. I promise you. Okay. So, what do dealers do? They hook you with a sample, a viral moment. You feel the high and when you don't have anymore, you experience withdrawals. You don't know what to do with yourself and then you're constantly checking the stats. It's like you scratching like, \"Oh, I need another fix.\" And now you have to chase your next fix. This is what's happening, right? So, the weird thing is the day before your post went viral, you were really happy with yourself, weren't you? You're making progress. I'm communicating. I'm becoming a better communicator. I feel better about myself. So what used to make you happy doesn't make you happy anymore. Weird because once you experience this feeling, everything else sucks in comparison. So you're chasing this thing one more time. So now you start to go in this negative spiral, this vicious cycle where you have more bad moments than good moments. Who still wants to go viral? Let's just keep checking the room. Okay, cool. I like that. You are resilient. &gt;&gt; No, I have a point. It's not a &gt;&gt; Well, wait. Can I finish the presentation before you make your point? There will be a point when you can make that point, but not right now cuz I'm worried about my time. Okay, cool. We're about halfway through. Okay, number three, the prison. Okay, what happens is when you go viral, well, what do you do? You try to repeat that. You might have been an experiment. You might have done something really goofy. You might have like put ice on your face or something. Now, every video you got to throw ice on your face. So, you become a caricature of yourself and you no longer feel like it's you who's showing up. So, you become a slave to the algorithm. This is why it's a prison. Brendan, hate me later, but I'm I'm gonna keep moving on. There's this idea. It's called the authenticity gap. Okay? It's the space that exists between the real you, who you are in private to your very best, most trusted friends, and how you show up in the world. Like, I don't sit in the hotel room with my skirt on. It's too uncomfortable. This is how I show up here. So, I am in some way operating as a persona or a character of myself. I'm wearing a hat. I have a mic. These aren't normal things I would do if I was sitting there in the living room watching TV. There's no need for that. So the bigger the gap between the real you and the persona who you show up to be in this world determines the lack of authenticity. So what's happening when you go viral? You show up even less of yourself. You become a prisoner of your own stupid success because this is all that people want from you now. So you have to maintain this image that you've created because you are so in love with this idea of attention and sooner than later you start wearing this mask and you can't even see yourself anymore. Your interests, your values and your personal growth take a backseat to keeping the relationship between you and the dealer happy and stable. It doesn't sound so good anymore, I don't think. And every time you try something different, you want to return back to the real you. You get punished for it, don't you? Oh my gosh, I'm shadowbanned now. What happened? Oh, you have fake followers cuz how did you get all that growth and now no one cares? Because you did something that they don't want to see from you. They'll leave comments like this. This isn't what we followed you for. Okay. So this avatar, this thing that you created to go viral becomes your prison. Okay. Number four, the awakening. What you start to realize is being viral doesn't mean you've done anything valuable. I'm sure you've scrolled through the feeds on Instagram, on Tik Tok. I think they call it brain rot for a reason. It's not necessarily valuable. It's just what the algorithm wants. And the algorithm, depending on the platform, rewards you every 10th post to give you an artificial boost. They give you a little love bomb. We love that you're on your 10th post. So, we're going to juice the numbers for you because they are like drug dealers. They want to keep encouraging you enough so you don't quit because the the platforms win when more people create. And you you start to understand that bigger reach doesn't mean that your message resonates anymore and that followers doesn't equal community. And I will go into that. Okay. Now, if you are here like many of us, you're trying to develop your personal brand, right? You guys are trying to develop your personal brand. What you need to understand what branding is. It's the stickiness of the relationship. It's how loyal people are to you. And there's simple tests. All you have to do is ask them to do something simple. Comment this. Go vote for me. Like, I'm I'm up for an award. Please vote for me. Please nom