ment. Okay? Many years ago I'm in Las Vegas at a conference, a technology conference, and I'm meeting up with a former student of mine. He's an entrepreneur. He's doing really well. We're meeting up at some Las Vegas hotel bar. We're sitting down and having a meal. And he says to me before the waiter comes out and takes our order. He goes, \"Chris, I noticed something about you. You seem really happy. Like you're almost glowing right now.\" I'm like, \"I am?\" He said, \"What's going on? We haven't seen each other in a couple of years.\" Former student of mine. I said, \"You know what, Greg? I think I've figured out the secret to my life.\" And I take out a little napkin. I said, \"You have a pen? Give me something here.\" It's the cliche napkin drawing, right? And I drew something like this. Not this. This is AI. Okay. I drew three circles. I think I finally figured out my life in my 40s. Something that I love to do, something I'm really good at, and what the world needs. Cuz right around here in about 20145, a former classmate of mine invited me to make content on YouTube. You're thinking, YouTube was that moment, that inflection point in your life? That sounds super shallow. Well, I'll tell you why. Because for 15 years, I taught at Art Center. It filled my soul with joy. It recharged me all the way. But the problem was when I was teaching at school, they paid me $58 an hour. That was less money than I paid freelancers to work for me. So, what they were doing is they were tapping into my passion for teaching and they were saying, \"Well, we'll pay you with passion.\" as the entrepreneur. I'm like, hm, that sounds like exploitation. And I did it for 15 years. So, if you want to criticize me for pursuing the money, you do something for 15 years where you don't get paid and tell me how you like it. On the other side, I was running my production company, making commercials and music videos for uh what Caitlyn was sharing with you all. I won't repeat that now. I made 