ld me who did different things to satisfy different needs versus one thing that put them all together. She goes, \"No.\" I was like, \"Well, let me tell you about it.\" Okay. The attention economy, as some people would argue, is the economy. Whoever can control attention controls opportunities. Mr. Beast is here. He's arguably the most successful person doing this. And underneath a subset, this is my theory here. Underneath the attention economy is the gift or giving economy. Okay? Bear with me. It's a concept. I'm working on it. If you don't like it, again, no refunds, okay? The gift or giving economy is built on on like five principles, okay? or five ideas, not some principles that you give freely with no short-term expectations where all you're doing is to do it to build relationships, community, and strengthen social bonds. No money, we're doing it because it feels good to give. Yes. Okay. So what you're doing is in lie of getting money, you trade money, for time, their time, their attention, perhaps some status because you're the educator, you're the thought leader. You trade it maybe for support because people who do good things get support from other people. They give money, they support you, introduce you to people and for connection. So what you're doing is instead of getting money, you're getting social currency. Now, I want to share with you four creators that highlight this concept of the giving or the gift economy. Okay, really quickly. The first one is a person named the trip man. Does anybody know the trip man? Trip man's awesome. You know the trip man and you're already smiling. Why? Because the trip man, he he's a former lab technician. He just quit his job and he wants to drive Ubers. It's crazy. Remember, he's a lab technician. Okay? So he rigs his Ubers with lights, microphones, funny party lights, and a whole bit. And when you get in the car, before he drives you anywhere, he says, \"Pick a song.\" And they pick a song, and they do karaoke. So on his account, he says he turns rides into singalong parties that pickups with personality, good vibes, and better playlists. That's on his bio. Okay. So his breakout moment was this song going to make you sweat. If you know that song, everybody dance now. 17 million views. Yeah. Wait a minute, Chris. Are you arguing against yourself now? Isn't that viral? It is viral as a byproduct of him being fully aligned with who he is. You all understand this? It's not like I think he woke up one day or maybe he did. Brandon's going to Brandon's going to break the door. I helped him. I coached it. What are you talking about? It could be. It's dangerous. I know. I'm going to roll the dice and hope it's not. Okay. But I love this thing because he's giving so freely. The amount of money he makes from an Uber fair is nothing compared to the amount of money that we support him with our views and then therefore the platforms give him money. Thousands of dollars versus hundreds of dollars or tens of dollars. Yes. Pretty cool. Yes. Okay. Next. Next one is a guy named Spencer B. SB mowing. Do you guys know Spencer? Yeah. You know everybody. You guys are really good. You're in the social media track. I understand. You got it. Okay. Um I'm I'm not sure about all these facts, but he has millions of followers. uh he was a Kansas state computer science grad and after working his miserable job for two to three years he quit and what he does he goes in and helps old people who can't afford lawn service with really nasty lawns to do the work for free and first they're a little skeptical like what free what do you mean no strings attached no strings attached remember the giftgiving economy no expectations so he does this and you know one of the videos get 100 million views that's a lot of money by doing good in the world. I think I'm in the right place to share this message with you. And the reaction, it's like sometimes it's like even a man like me might tear up a little bit or maybe got dusty