m and all of a sudden you speak Arabic or Farsy or whatever it is and they're like, \"Wow.\" And you film that whole thing. Yeah, it's a $300 color job, but it's a $3,000 giving act. And it's really cool to see that. So eventually you can do this where you charge nothing and you just go to find really interesting people who are transforming the process when they see themselves differently. Isn't that wonderful? I don't know if she's going to do this, but it's my wish for her. And it's also my wish for all of you. Okay, remember how I said before like branding is about building trust at scale. Building trust at scale. I want to just introduce really quickly to this idea. Uh it it's a Francis Frey and Anne Morris wrote about this. It's called beginning with trust. If you want to have high trust factor, number one, you have to show up authentically as yourself. Are you who you pretend to be online? So we have to close the authenticity gap. Stop stop performing. Stop becoming a character or an avatar of yourself. Be the real you. Share real opinions and ideas. Okay. Number two, it's empathy. It's not empathy the way you think. We need to feel like you care about your audience more than your need to make money. This is really important. So, car salespeople not great. Time share salespeople not great at that. We know what they want. They want to meet their numbers more than they want to take care of us. Yes. Last one is you have to be logical. You have to be able to explain ideas in ways that are easy to follow. So I think the pursuit for you is to become a better educator to teach people. And my saying is this. If you want to reach more people, teach more people. Okay. Uh this is a quote from Alex Ramosi. You cannot wish for both strong character and an easy life. The price of each is the other. You want an easy life or you want to be have to have strong character. Okay, this is my full circle moment. Okay, it looks like I'm I'm like antiviral and like I don't want to be successful at all. I started a company, a YouTube channel called The Future in 2014. So, some quick stats for you. We've made over 2,000 videos, a gazillion hours, over a million dollars in revenue, and we've had 24 videos that have had million plus views. So, there's nothing wrong with going viral. Okay, that should not be the goal, though. Here's our number one highest performing video. Has 4 and a half million views. And the shorts, two short versions, one has 50 million and the recut of the same video has 44 million. Pretty cool. But the part I want to show to you is this part. Look at the gap between when we released it and when we went viral. We are the classic example of an overnight success story 10 years in the making. So you remember my friend Jody Cook. It's a series that I do and the format's called Man on the Street where I go up to people and ask them how much do you charge an hour? And do you know who told me to say that? Mr. Brendan Kane told me to say that. Okay. So, I'm going to end it with this quote before I get out of here. The person who loves walking will walk further than the person who loves the destination. I want to encourage all of you to just learn to love walking, to take that step, and keep taking that step because one day when you look up, you'll be surprised how far you've come. I'm out of time, everybody. Thank you very much. [applause and cheering] &gt;&gt; Thank you. &gt;&gt; Thank you, Craig. I got to give you this.","uploadedAt":"2026-08-03T13:53:35.478Z","chunks":[{"id":"doc_1785765215478_14t4u0_c0","text":"Uh about a week ago, I was asked to speak on a topic and I've put together a brand new presentation for some of you. So bear with me if there are some typos and some weird things. And the reason why I did this is because they asked me to talk about the topic of virality. And nobody does a better job than that than Brendan Kaine. He's our friend. Uh we he's been on multiple podcasts with us. So Brendan, with all apologies, I'm going to take a little different approach and talk to you about something because when I think of Mind Valley, I think of a more spiritual personal uh pursuit versus this thing. There's this promise of social media that anyone can go viral. And I wonder why or where we got this idea from that anyone can go viral, right? And I think it's the social media networks have told us this and they make this promise like they dangle that little carrot for us to pursue and it keeps us chasing. So I want to ask you this question. If anyone can go viral. The question is should you? So I want you to think about this. Okay. Some of you may know this person. His name is Mark Manson. Do you know Mark Manson? Anybody? Okay, great. Well read group here. I love that. He wrote this book called The Subtle Art of Not Giving an F. And I heard him say this thing about if you want to be a 1enter, and I don't think he's talking about the top 1% of the richest people in the world, but the people who make the greatest impact in the world, the top 1enter, they have something in common. They all do this one thing. They are contrarians. When everyone's going one direction, they go the opposite direction. But any fool can go a different direction. So you have to be a correct contrarian. You have to find something that goes against what the mainstream is saying, whatever it is that you strongly believe in your heart. Not to do it because it's it's a gimmick, but because you believe in your heart, this isn't correct. Because if you say what everyone else is saying, you're going to disappear in all the noise. So, you have to become a correct contrarian. And I take this very personally because I am not trying to be contrarian, but I have very strong points of view on lots of things. So, I'm going to use this opportunity to probably undo everything that Brendan just said. Okay. So, we're gonna have a little bit of fun together. Okay. So, from the people who are laughing, I presume you were here for that. And for the people who are not laughing, maybe you're going to be mad at me or you're just new here. So, it's okay. This presentation exists with or without Brendan. He's a very important","t0":0,"t1":200,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"uh":0.08825664271133458,"week":0.06470352276358199,"ago":0.05419448468724774,"asked":0.09366707403825238,"speak":0.052408736376969335,"topic":0.15042512167983246,"ve":0.033176408534579274,"put":0.033176408534579274,"together":0.082159933863928,"brand":0.04804709149394142,"presentation":0.1226407363131379,"some":0.10210665904412836,"bear":0.06906516764660989,"me":0.07170203736307176,"there":0.039336135346302786,"typos":0.07521256083991623,"weird":0.05419448468724774,"things":0.054058030682545895,"reason":0.045709961070228525,"why":0.0573935519936313,"because":0.14187516440555947,"talk":0.0738382747937749,"virality":0.06132036815656895,"nobody":0.04804709149394142,"better":0.04030229200390048,"job":0.04804709149394142,"brendan":0.25881409105432795,"kaine":0.07521256083991623,"he":0.10233108531817021,"friend":0.04030229200390048,"multiple":0.052408736376969335,"podcasts":0.07521256083991623,"us":0.12390102055987315,"apologies":0.07521256083991623,"going":0.12763332380516043,"take":0.06731552250552039,"little":0.09417198067290257,"different":0.0636355367737625,"approach":0.05621899914656276,"something":0.07018576324519485,"think":0.11950339560511958,"mind":0.04466393688692839,"valley":0.07521256083991623,"spiritual":0.06906516764660989,"personal":0.041079966931964,"pursuit":0.05419448468724774,"versus":0.04936630031351652,"thing":0.07832959503692624,"promise":0.1226407363131379,"social":0.09141992214045705,"media":0.10481747275393867,"anyone":0.18396110446970682,"go":0.14709440451889097,"viral":0.14050061105737857,"wonder":0.06132036815656895,"where":0.02641009932055321,"got":0.03753805341760719,"idea":0.04189969830063509,"right":0.02552666476103209,"networks":0.07521256083991623,"told":0.04466393688692839,"make":0.05282019864110642,