989612892,"scarcity":0.12016172942855916,"problem":0.04190183683643367,"real":0.03559025120696515,"thing":0.025582091475825176,"example":0.04478599965582871,"daniel":0.07369224661371263,"priestley":0.07369224661371263,"talks":0.05737250125310413,"oversubscribed":0.07369224661371263}},{"id":"doc_1785765215613_7k08ba_c7","text":"think. If not in that book, at least in conversation. He says we all have finite amounts of clients that we can take on. So for you, how many clients can you work with in a month? Hugo, at any point in time, can only work with two to three clients. Yet he does not communicate that to his audience. The to say, \"I can only take on three clients a month.\" That creates scarcity already. We do not have unlimited capacity. You cannot infinitely scale creative businesses, but what we don't do is to communicate that to the outside world. Therefore, when someone gets to work with you, they're like, \"Wow, I'm glad I got my money in before the next batch of clients come to you cuz I really do want to work with you.\" That's real scarcity, and that's a real concept that you can use because we want what we can't have, and also we're very aware that if we don't get in on this round, we might have to wait two, three, four, six months, and our problem can't wait that long. But if you are always available 24/7, 7 days a week, basically available at any point in time, there's no urgency for them to make a decision because there's no penalty for them to wait. So, if there's no penalty, they just wait. &gt;&gt; So, from a creative and I want to communicate to people that I have a finite amount of resources. What would that post or how would you communicate this in in your marketing? &gt;&gt; Very simple. The way you would market is at any one time, I can only process six or seven clients. I actually have an opening in June. If you're interested in working with me, get on the wait list now. Reach out, let me know. &gt;&gt; So, is this something putting on your website and making a post about it? &gt;&gt; single where every single place you could you could write and tell people this because they need to know, right? Restaurants do this all the time. Concerts do this all the time. They announce when the tickets are going to go on sale. They have a history of selling out, and they tell you in advance when you can book or when you can buy tickets. And probably for their loyal customers, they might give you a little lead time. But maybe you're a sneakerhead, and they do this exact same thing. They have limited drops. I wouldn't consider a drop. It would just be a release where only the people in the know, loyal customers, or people who are in the VIP club have access to this, and they know they will sell out. People wait in line for them, and then they could immediately turn around and flip the shoes. It's a kind of wink-wink relationship they have with their die-hard fans. Basically, we'll give you early access to a finite quantity","t0":1400,"t1":1600,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"24":0.0629034390533557,"think":0.023235750515725236,"book":0.07424207114113864,"least":0.054654959041069276,"conversation":0.049397733978109634,"he":0.03979367375155209,"says":0.04342136427587442,"finite":0.20143122251514695,"amounts":0.07312011054061755,"clients":0.3057041978933116,"take":0.06544277168250401,"many":0.02755956267309197,"work":0.09001885741596068,"month":0.11922881093074293,"hugo":0.07312011054061755,"point":0.08684272855174884,"time":0.14562317451064638,"only":0.14125573432781688,"yet":0.04671039786385865,"communicate":0.26857496335352926,"his":0.025097262094692385,"audience":0.05268676756609387,"say":0.025097262094692385,"creates":0.06714374083838232,"scarcity":0.11922881093074293,"already":0.050950699648885274,"unlimited":0.07312011054061755,"capacity":0.06714374083838232,"cannot":0.05692706935112049,"infinitely":0.07312011054061755,"scale":0.059614405465371466,"creative":0.0783621249816956,"businesses":0.07312011054061755,"don"