 I can look up to that. But the rest of it like why do we believe someone's in or out of our league? That's a value statement that's just full of judgment that that really bothers me. So it's these kind of beliefs. I can't wear that. You shouldn't wear that. It's really a a projection of your own insecurity and your own prejudice. And I think people need to check themselves. Let's get into your question now. Your question is why do people talk so much about what they do versus who they are? Because there's a lot of templates out there that exist. We don't know what to talk about. We're told from very young age don't talk about yourself. Don't think about yourself so much. So what do we do? We deflect. We're like, well, nobody's going to complain if I I tell people I'm a plumber or I'm a roofing contractor or I build websites. But you know what? So does everybody else who does that exact same thing. Why should I care about you? What we're realizing is in the 21st century we have an embarrassment of choices. Where we're kind of inundated and overwhelmed with how many choices we have in the world. In fact, there's a cognitive bias. It's called the paradox of choice. That the more options that we have, the harder it is for us to make a decision. And the reason why potentially some of your clients don't choose to follow you or to reach out to you is because they can't tell why you're different than anyone else and so therefore they shut down. And you and I both know this. Anytime you need to do something you don't know about you start to do too much research and you're like, \"I'm just overwhelmed.\" That's why it just takes us too long to do something. Steven Bartlett has had this great um podcast or No, it was a keynote talk. He goes something like, \"Back when I was younger, in my 20s, I was speaking and some notable person spoke before me, probably head of state, maybe even a former president or something.\" And he says, \"There are two doors, door one and door two. 