ow well, who the heck are you? And there's a good chance you don't even know who you are. Hence, the questions I pose in the book and the workshops, well, who are you? What are your beliefs? What are your values? And what people tend to do is they want to talk about all that they're good at, all the strengths and the beautiful things. We're very used to that. This is why people appear too perfect on TV or on on social media. It's because they're presenting their very best self on their very best day. That's really boring to me. &gt;&gt; Mhm. &gt;&gt; And it reeks of inauthenticity. So, I say to people, look into your shadow self, the things that you feel great vulnerability around, things that you might bring anxiety or make you nervous. Let's figure out how you can incorporate that into you because that makes you much more interesting. Earlier today, we're talking to manage to John. He's shared this very vulnerable story about how he bought a U-bake pizza and he didn't know he had to bake it even though it was in the title. Now, I'm ribbing him a little bit, but that story's endearing. Now, people on the other side of the camera are going to say, \"Chris, why you give John such a hard time? He's just an earnest guy trying to make it in the world and yeah, he didn't have your upbringing, blah blah blah.\" But they're going to connect to that story. &gt;&gt; Mhm. &gt;&gt; Despite how he might feel in that moment, those little quirks and the weird things allow people to say, \"You're not a perfect person. I'm not a perfect person. We might have something in common.\" I don't know about you. I'm not perfect and when I see perfect people, which they don't exist, but they present themselves to be, there's something about it feels like they're driving a wedge between me and them. My ability to connect with them is not going to be great. &gt;&gt; I hear you on doing the deep work and trying to find out more by yourself before you make that first post, but I think for some 