cubism was an entirely new way of looking at the world instead of portraying subjects\n  - [≈10:21] be willing to destroy what came before this philosophy permeated every aspect of his work whether it was cubism surrealism or classical art Picasso always sought to dismantle the traditional forms in\n  QUOTE: \"be willing to destroy what came before this philosophy permeated every aspect of his work whether it was cubism surrealism or classical art Picasso always sought to dismantle the traditional forms in\" (≈10:21)\n[10:40–16:00] War · History · Spain\n  - [≈11:32] war the chaotic composition features distorted figures crying women and slain animals all tangled in a scene of chaos and Devastation the mural sized painting is a raw depiction of the trauma\n  - [≈12:08] and his dictatorship in Spain despite receiving multiple invitations to return to his homeland Picasso refused to do so until Spain was was liberated from Franco's rule his opposition to Fascism was\n  - [≈12:46] Decades of his life he remained as prolific as ever he continued to create often working on multiple paintings sculptures and prints at the same time his passion for art never faded\n  - [≈14:19] and innovating until the very end on April 8th 1973 at the age of 91 Pablo Picasso passed away in muan France his death marked the end of an era\n  QUOTE: \"and his dictatorship in Spain despite receiving multiple invitations to return to his homeland Picasso refused to do so until Spain was was liberated from Franco's rule his opposition to Fascism was\" (≈12:08)\n[16:00–17:13] Challenge · Experience · Itself\n  - [≈16:17] Norms of his time to dismantle reality and to reassemble it in a way that forced the world to look at itself differently from the cubis movement to the surrealist experiment from\n  - [≈16:30] his depictions of love to the horrors of War Picasso's work covers the entire spectrum of human emotion and experience he taught us that art is about exploring about going beyond what\n  - [