"answers":0.13070884129198249,"source":0.46352906151726625,"teach":0.4518214274166231,"cubism":0.11588226537931656,"apply":0.12640188840143252,"today":0.09053606644453466}}]},{"id":"doc_1787410478461_jpjk55","name":"DIGEST_picasso_untold_story","text":"DIGEST · TURTLE · picasso_untold_story · runtime ≈45:48 · mode:estimate\nTITLE: He's a very special figure, a genius unequivocally. [music] &gt;&gt; When we sat in\nTHESIS: &gt;&gt; Just off 5th Avenue, on a site long occupied by the old-fashioned town dwellings of the Rockefeller family, stands the spanking new home of a nationally important institution, the New York Museum of Modern Art.\n\nCHAPTERS:\n[0:00–5:20] Young · Pepe · Fine\n  - [≈0:52] &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Pablo Picasso was born on the 25th [music] of October to father Don Jose Ruiz Blasco, nicknamed Pepe, and his mother [music] Maria Picasso y Lopez.\n  - [≈1:41] He began by drawing animals such as donkeys [music] and dogs, completing the images with a single line, beginning at the head and ending at the tail.\n  - [≈2:35] In 1892, Picasso [music] began studying at the School of Fine Arts in La Coruña, where his father Don José taught.\n  - [≈4:45] In 1899, Picasso attended [music] a cafe named Els Quatre Gats to meet a circle of avant-garde artists.\n  QUOTE: \"In 1892, Picasso [music] began studying at the School of Fine Arts in La Coruña, where his father Don José taught.\" (≈2:35)\n[5:20–10:40] Carles · Guitarist · Blue\n  - [≈5:25] The two settled in a vacant studio whilst attending exhibitions such as the Universal, which raked in over 32 million visitors, all pining to see [music] the next influx of creative talent.\n  - [≈6:23] On the 17th of February, 1901, in a Paris cafe, Carles put a gun to his temple and ended his own life in attendance of his friends.\n  - [≈8:30] The continued use of blue sets a melodramatic tone for the painting conveying a sense of sadness, despair, and isolation reflecting the emotional state of the subject alongside the artist himself during this period of his life.\n  - [≈10:19] The old guitarist's world-weary expression and the stark [music] simplicity of the composition invite contemplation of the human condition and the universal themes of suffering, resilience, [music] and the redemptive power of art.\n  QUOTE: \"The two settled in a vacant studio whilst attending exhibitions such as the Universal, which raked in over 32 million visitors, all pining to see [music] the next influx of creative talent.\" (≈5:25)\n[10:40–16:00] Her · She · Salon\n  - [≈11:40] His desire was to expand and fundamentally break the [music] rules of what had been traditionally depicted.\n  - [≈13:52] Gertrude sat for hours in a broken arm chair in her home number 27 Rue de Fleurus.\n  - [≈14:09] On the 11th of May, 1906, [music] they ventured to Gósol, a small village northwest of Comarca Berguedà in Catalonia.\n  - [≈14:23] Its archaic minimalism served as the core inspiration [music] for Gertrude's mask-like face alongside ethnographic motifs [music] inspired by Iberian sculptures Picasso had observed at the Louvre.\n  QUOTE: \"Gertrude sat for hours in a broken arm chair in her home number 27 Rue de Fleurus.\" (≈13:52)\n[16:00–21:20] Said · Me · Picture\n  - [≈17:09] &gt;&gt; Well, we knew when it was bought that there was something on the &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; back, but we didn't know how important, how brilliant a picture it was until we removed the backing of the frame and saw that it was picture painted in oil 4 y…\n  - [≈17:52] &gt;&gt; Just off 5th Avenue, on a site long occupied by the old-fashioned town dwellings of the Rockefeller family, stands the spanking new home of a nationally important institution, the New York Museum of Modern Art.\n  - [≈18:32] &gt;&gt; Well, I think we went started initially in in the '50s, '54, something like this, but in 1963, uh &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; we went as usual during the summer holidays with the children, and so on.\n  - [≈19:36] And he took [music] something out of his white coat, which was a little medallion like this, and with a with a ribbon, he pinned it round Sheila's neck, [music] and said that he much preferred to give presents to women than he did to men.\n  QUOTE: \"&gt;&gt; Just off 5th Avenue, on a site long occupied by the old-fashioned town dwellings of the Rockefeller family, stands the spanking new home of a nationally important institution, the New York Museum of Modern Art.\" (≈17:52)\n[21:20–26:40] Humor · Jacqueline · Last\n  - [≈22:46] &gt;&gt; Picasso bought his first painting in 1908, a portrait of a woman made in 1895 by French artist [music] Henri Rousseau.\n  - [≈23:39] Recent art frauds and record-breaking high prices for master works have kept all kinds of art in the world news spotlight.\n  - [≈23:56] In 1909, the two temporarily [music] settled in a Paris studio, and together they visited Horta, a neighborhood in Barcelona.\n  - [≈25:30] It's very much a a paean to uh to Jacqueline, um his last uh and and longest-lasting muse of his life, um who he would marry about 5 years after this picture was painted.\n  QUOTE: \"It's very much a a paean to uh to Jacqueline, um his last uh and and longest-lasting muse of his life, um who he would marry about 5 years after this picture was painted.\" (≈25:30)\n[26:40–32:00] Company · Influence · Things\n  - [≈26:42] He continued to develop on Cubism, working alongside Braque [music] on Analytic Cubism to Synthetic, effectively incorporating real-world materials into their artwork [music] to create depth in its messaging.\n  - [≈29:05] His father was a great Anglophile and was known as El Inglés because of his passion for all things English and Picasso told [music] his biographer Roland Penrose that he was on his way to London in 1900 when he [music] stopped off in Paris and and never carri…\n  - [≈29:47] &gt;&gt; There are about a dozen works by Picasso, including the most important, uh a portrait of Serge Lifar executed by Picasso in 1960, estimated [music] at uh 300 to 500,000 francs.\n  - [≈30:24] This collaboration marked an important intersection of visual art, music, and dance, showcasing Picasso's innovative approach to stage design, and his influence on avant-garde performance art.\n  QUOTE: \"&gt;&gt; There are about a dozen works by Picasso, including the most important, uh a portrait of Serge Lifar executed by Picasso in 1960, estimated [music] at uh 300 to 500,000 francs.\" (≈29:47)\n[32:00–37:20] Looking · Studies · Color\n  - [≈35:40] He painted it in 1955 &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and it is simply the single most important work by Picasso remaining in private hands.\n  - [≈35:56] [music] Matisse dies and Picasso, over a period of 2 months, starts his Herculean project of Les Femmes d'Alger, 80 [music] studies on paper, 14 studies in oil, with and without color, looking on its form and color, looking at changing the persons around the…\n  - [≈36:27] It's estimated at around $140 million, which is which in itself is already the most expensive work of art ever sold at auction.\n  - [≈37:08] When a major work of art by a major artist comes up, There's sufficient global interest and the various economies are still strong enough to mean that they're pushed to to price levels which we haven't seen before.\n  QUOTE: \"When a major work of art by a major artist comes up, There's sufficient global interest and the various economies are still strong enough to mean that they're pushed to to price levels which we haven't seen before.\" (≈37:08)\n[37:20–42:40] War · Guernica · Spanish\n  - [≈39:21] Though he was being watched, the artist continued to resist [music] alongside his fellow Parisians, signing partitions against fascism and oppression, and taking part in secret resistance gatherings who were opposed to the Nazi atrocities.\n  - [≈40:12] He never visited Guernica itself, the Basque town obliterated by German bombers in the Spanish Civil War.\n  - [≈40:40] Measuring 3.49 m tall and 7.76 m wide, the painting is rendered in a stark monochromatic palette of black, white, and grays, which enhances [music] its dramatic impact and evokes the feel of newspaper photographs, contemporary accounts of the bombing, and per…\n  - [≈41:29] Initially [music] displayed at the Spanish Pavilion of the 1937 World's Fair in Paris, it became a touring [music] exhibition to raise awareness and funds for Spanish war relief.\n  QUOTE: \"He never visited Guernica itself, the Basque town obliterated by German bombers in the Spanish Civil War.\" (≈40:12)\n[42:40–45:47] Career · Death · Just\n  - [≈42:44] This was one of 120 figures he created, [music] most of which featured nude females, with some animals such as owls, bulls, and monkeys, &gt;&gt; [music] [music] &gt;&gt; Pablo Picasso [music] produced around 13,500 paintings in his impressive 78-year career.\n  - [≈44:01] The show of late Picasso at the Tate offers a careful [music] selection from the last 20 years of his life, from 1953 to '73.\n  - [≈44:21] \"If I spit,\" he said, \"they'll take it, frame it, and sell it as great art.\" &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Picasso died at the Mas Notre Dame de Vie in Mougins on the 8th of April, 1978.\n  - [≈44:48] He didn't just do paintings, so it's basically always constructions, going into ceramics.\n  QUOTE: \"\"If I spit,\" he said, \"they'll take it, frame it, and sell it as great art.\" &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Picasso died at the Mas Notre Dame de Vie in Mougins on the 8th of April, 1978.\" (≈44:21)\n\nTAKEAWAYS & HEURISTICS:\n  - &gt;&gt; Just off 5th Avenue, on a site long occupied by the old-fashioned town dwellings of the Rockefeller family, stands the spanking new home of a nationally important institution, the New York Museum of Modern Art.\n  - His father was a great Anglophile and was known as El Inglés because of his passion for all things English and Picasso told [music] his biographer Roland Penrose that he was on his way to London in 1900 when he [music]…\n  -