344770969521,"spanish":0.04238918914410415,"civil":0.04959708750614251,"war":0.039928408620320745,"questions":0.04109717635514436,"answers":0.04383353655869704,"source":0.11658422182770455,"teach":0.07575972237573514}},{"id":"doc_1787410478461_jpjk55_c4","text":"teach about his? - What does the source teach about picasso? - How does picasso untold story apply today?","t0":2722,"t1":2747,"tsReal":false,"tfIdfVector":{"teach":0.5794201429578215,"his":0.1596932345688126,"source":0.29721706275097065,"picasso":0.39364645680715155,"untold":0.4272338996610687,"story":0.23220864429592156,"apply":0.3241979941786268,"today":0.23220864429592156}}]},{"id":"doc_video_perception_turtle","name":"video_perception_routing","text":"VIDEO PERCEPTION ROUTING - how an agent looks at a video and turns it into art that matches it.\n\nFOUR CAPABILITIES: perception (eyes on video), transcription (speech to timecodes), frame selection (which frames to look at), and beat detection (BPM and cut points). Classify the need before picking a tool. Understanding what a video looks like needs perception and frames; understanding when things happen needs transcription and beats. Matching a video to art needs both: palette and composition come from frames, cuts and easing come from the beat grid and word timings.\n\nCONTACT SHEET FIRST: before spending a frame budget, build one montage of evenly spaced stills. One image answers what the video looks like, whether deeper analysis is warranted, and where to look. When you do not know where the interesting parts are, generate the contact sheet first and choose frames from it, never blindly by interval. It doubles as the storyboard you hand a human.\n\nFRAME SELECTION: never sample at a fixed interval when content varies, that yields duplicates from static shots and missed cuts from fast ones. Select by content instead: deduplicate near-identical frames with SSIM, or key frames off optical flow so still scenes are skipped and action is sampled densely. Useful signals are cumulative motion displacement and the survival ratio of tracked points; the second is a guardrail, because selecting frames after you lost your tracks is how you get redundant stills. Ask for a frame count when coverage matters (video-to-keyframes --target-frames N), set motion thresholds when explainability matters (DIS optical flow with in_bounds_ratio). Extract many, view few: plan with cheap signals, then show the agent only the chosen stills.\n\nAGENT VISION TOOLS: prefer MCP tools over uploading video, do not upload video when frames do. Three shapes - a server that acquires the URL itself with yt-dlp covering a thousand plus platforms; a perception-only server that makes zero LLM calls, best when cost or privacy matter; a batch CLI writing deterministic files another tool can path to. Escalate analysis modes cheapest first: evenly spaced overview of about 14 frames, then scene-change detection, then full mode with forced speech recognition. Plan with cheap analytical signals - scene changes, silence, motion, blur, exposure, loudness - before committing frames. bengemine frame_mode is a host contract: images inline, paths as timestamp to absolute path list, descriptions with no server-side LLM call.\n\nBURN TIMESTAMPS: overlay source timecode directly onto extracted pixels rather than tracking it in a filename. Once frames are reordered, gridded, montaged, or passed between agents, pixels still carry when each occurred. Cheapest correctness guarantee in a frame pipeline.\n\nBEAT SYNCED MOTION DESIGN: derive beat times as numbers, then map to frames with cut_frames = round(beat_time * fps). Detect beats from the audio track, not the visuals. Nudge onsets to the preceding energy minimum when they mark cut points rather than attack timings. Neither librosa nor beatlyzer detects downbeats, so derive them yourself, beat_times[i % 4] for 4/4, and state the assumption. Prefer landing within a frame or two of the beat over a visible stutter.\n\nMAKING ART THAT MATCHES A VIDEO: read two different things. From frames take static qualities - dominant palette, contrast, grain, composition, focal lengths, and the mood those imply. From timing take dynamic qualities - cut points, beat grid, speech word timings, and the pacing they imply. Frames alone cannot tell you when things happen and audio alone cannot tell you what it looks like. Align the art to the beat grid while deriving its palette from representative stills chosen via contact sheet.\n\nWINDOWS THIS MACHINE: ffmpeg 8.1.2, ffprobe, opencv 4.13, python 3.12, node 25 installed. No whisper, librosa, beatlyzer, decord, or video MCP server installed yet. The full routing reference with commands lives in shaddai-design-studio tools/VIDEO.md.","uploadedAt":1791306775299,"chunks":[],"kind":"knowledge","tsMode":"none"}]